         ('Harvey v Facey (1893)', 'in reply to a telegram asking for the lowest price of a property, the defendant replied "lowest price £900"', 'a statement of price in response to a request for information is not an offer'),
         ('Hyde v Wrench (1840)', 'an offer to sell a farm for £1 000 was met with an offer to buy it for £950, which was refused, and the buyer then tried to accept at £1 000', 'a counter-offer rejects and destroys the original offer, which can no longer be accepted'),
         ('Stevenson, Jacques & Co v McLean (1880)', 'the buyer asked whether he could have credit terms on an offer to sell iron', 'a mere request for information does not reject the offer'),
