T(3, 'Misrepresentation: types and remedies', area='misrepresentation',
  defs=[('misrepresentation', 'An untrue statement of fact (not opinion or intention) made by one party to the other before the contract, which induces the other party to enter into the contract.'),
        ('fraudulent misrepresentation', 'A false statement made knowingly, without belief in its truth, or recklessly as to whether it is true or false (Derry v Peek).'),
        ('negligent misrepresentation', 'A false statement made carelessly; under s2(1) Misrepresentation Act 1967 the representor is liable unless they prove they had reasonable grounds to believe, and did believe, it was true.'),
        ('innocent misrepresentation', 'A false statement which the maker had reasonable grounds to believe was true; damages may be awarded in lieu of rescission under s2(2) Misrepresentation Act 1967.'),
        ('rescission', 'An equitable remedy that sets a contract aside and restores the parties to their pre-contract positions; it can be barred by affirmation, delay, third-party rights or impossibility of restoration.')],
  mcqs=[('In Bisset v Wilkinson (1927), the statement that land could support 2 000 sheep was:', 'an opinion, not a statement of fact', ['a fraudulent misrepresentation', 'a term of the contract', 'a half-truth'], 'Neither party had experience of sheep farming on that land.'),
        ('Which case shows that a statement of intention can be a misrepresentation if the person never had that intention?', 'Edgington v Fitzmaurice (1885)', ['Bisset v Wilkinson (1927)', 'Attwood v Small (1838)', 'Fletcher v Krell (1873)'], 'A statement of intention is a statement of the state of one\'s mind.'),
        ('Under s2(1) Misrepresentation Act 1967, the burden of proof is on:', 'the representor to show reasonable grounds for believing the statement was true', ['the claimant to prove fraud', 'the court to investigate', 'nobody, as liability is strict'], 'Howard Marine v Ogden (1978).'),
        ('In Royscot Trust v Rogerson (1991), damages under s2(1) were assessed:', 'on the same basis as for fraud, covering all direct losses', ['on a contract basis only', 'at a nominal level', 'only for foreseeable losses'], 'The "fiction of fraud".'),
        ('Which case shows that rescission may be barred by lapse of time?', 'Leaf v International Galleries (1950)', ['Long v Lloyd (1958)', 'Redgrave v Hurd (1881)', 'With v O\'Flanagan (1936)'], 'Five years after buying a painting falsely said to be a Constable.')],
  cases=[('Bisset v Wilkinson (1927)', 'a seller of land in New Zealand, which had never been used for sheep, said it could support 2 000 sheep', 'a statement of honest opinion by someone with no special knowledge is not a misrepresentation'),
         ('Esso Petroleum Co Ltd v Mardon (1976)', 'Esso\'s expert told a prospective tenant that a petrol station would sell 200 000 gallons a year', 'an opinion given by someone with special expertise may be treated as a statement of fact'),
         ('Edgington v Fitzmaurice (1885)', 'company directors said money raised from debentures would improve the business, but intended to pay off debts', 'a false statement of intention is a misrepresentation of fact'),
         ('Dimmock v Hallett (1866)', 'a seller said farms were fully let, but did not say the tenants had given notice to leave', 'a half-truth can be a misrepresentation'),
         ('With v O\'Flanagan (1936)', 'a doctor selling his practice gave true figures for income, which fell sharply before the sale', 'a statement that becomes untrue before the contract must be corrected'),
         ('Attwood v Small (1838)', 'buyers of a mine had the owner\'s exaggerated claims checked by their own surveyors', 'there is no misrepresentation if the claimant did not rely on the statement'),
         ('Redgrave v Hurd (1881)', 'a solicitor exaggerated the income of his practice and the buyer did not check the accounts', 'a claimant who relied on the statement can claim even if they could have discovered the truth'),
         ('Derry v Peek (1889)', 'directors stated a company had permission to use steam trams, believing permission would be granted', 'fraud requires a false statement made knowingly, without belief in its truth, or recklessly'),
         ('Howard Marine & Dredging Co Ltd v A Ogden & Sons Ltd (1978)', 'a barge owner wrongly stated the barges\' capacity, relying on a Lloyd\'s register entry', 'under s2(1) the representor must prove reasonable grounds for believing the statement, which they could not'),
         ('Long v Lloyd (1958)', 'a lorry buyer continued to use the lorry after discovering serious defects', 'affirmation of the contract bars rescission'),
         ('Leaf v International Galleries (1950)', 'a buyer tried to rescind five years after buying a painting falsely described as a Constable', 'lapse of time can bar rescission')],
  ars=[('A statement of opinion is not usually a misrepresentation.', 'A misrepresentation must be a false statement of fact.', 'A'),
       ('Silence is always a misrepresentation.', 'There is generally no duty to disclose information in contract negotiations.', 'D'),
       ('A claimant who could easily have discovered the truth cannot claim misrepresentation.', 'In Redgrave v Hurd (1881) the claimant succeeded despite not checking the accounts.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain the requirements of an actionable misrepresentation.', 'A false statement of existing fact (not opinion, Bisset v Wilkinson; not intention unless false, Edgington); made by one party to the other; which induced the claimant to enter the contract (Attwood v Small; Redgrave v Hurd).', '1 mark false statement of fact; 1 mark made by party; 1 mark inducement with case. [3]'),
        ('Explain the remedies available for each type of misrepresentation.', 'Fraudulent: rescission and damages in the tort of deceit. Negligent: rescission and damages under s2(1) MA 1967 on the fraud measure (Royscot). Innocent: rescission, or damages in lieu at the court\'s discretion under s2(2).', '1 mark each type. [3]'),
        ('Explain three bars to rescission.', 'Affirmation (Long v Lloyd); lapse of time (Leaf v International Galleries); third-party rights acquired in good faith; restitution impossible (the subject matter cannot be returned).', '1 mark per bar, up to 3. [3]')],
  cmps=[('negligent misrepresentation under s2(1) MA 1967', 'fraudulent misrepresentation', ['for s2(1) the burden is on the representor to disprove negligence, for fraud the claimant must prove dishonesty (Derry v Peek)', 'damages are assessed on the same basis for both (Royscot Trust v Rogerson)'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A} bought a {biz} in {city} from {B}. Before the sale {B} said that the business {stmt}. In fact {truth}. {A} relied on the statement. {A} has {now}.',
             slots={'biz': ['café', 'hair salon', 'small hotel'],
                    'stmt': [('made a profit of $50 000 a year', 'it had made a loss for two years', 'a false statement of fact'), ('"will certainly be popular with tourists"', 'tourist numbers were low', 'probably an opinion (Bisset v Wilkinson) unless {B} had special knowledge (Esso v Mardon)')],
                    'truth': ['{stmt_1}'],
                    'now': ['run the business for three years', 'just found out the truth after two weeks']},
             issue='misrepresentation', wrong=['breach of a condition', 'common mistake', 'undue influence'],
             iq='Which vitiating factor is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {A} on whether there has been an actionable misrepresentation and what remedies are available.',
             law=['a misrepresentation is a false statement of fact which induces the contract', 'opinions are not usually statements of fact (Bisset v Wilkinson) unless made by an expert (Esso v Mardon)', 'types: fraudulent (Derry v Peek), negligent under s2(1) MA 1967 (Howard Marine), innocent', 'rescission may be barred by affirmation or lapse of time (Long v Lloyd; Leaf v International Galleries); damages under s2(1) on the fraud measure (Royscot)'],
             apply=['{B}\'s statement was {stmt_2}', '{A} relied on it, so inducement is satisfied (Redgrave v Hurd)', '{A} has {now}, which affects whether rescission is still available'],
             concl='if the statement was a false statement of fact, {A} can probably claim damages under s2(1) MA 1967; rescission depends on whether it is barred by delay or affirmation'),
        dict(facts='{A} bought a {item} from {B}, a dealer, for {p} after {B} said it was {desc}. {B} {belief}. The statement was untrue.',
             slots={'item': ['painting', 'antique clock', 'vintage guitar'], 'p': ['$20 000', '$8 000', '$35 000'],
                    'desc': ['made by a famous artist', 'over 200 years old', 'owned by a famous musician'],
                    'belief': [('knew this was false', 'fraudulent (Derry v Peek): rescission and damages in deceit'), ('relied on an old catalogue without checking', 'negligent under s2(1) MA 1967, as {B} cannot show reasonable grounds (Howard Marine)'), ('had a recent expert certificate confirming it', 'probably innocent: rescission or damages in lieu under s2(2)')]},
             issue='the type of misrepresentation and the remedies available', wrong=['the postal rule', 'economic duress', 'frustration'],
             iq='Which vitiating factor is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {A} which type of misrepresentation has occurred and what remedies are available.',
             law=['fraudulent: knowingly, without belief or recklessly (Derry v Peek)', 'negligent under s2(1) MA 1967: liable unless reasonable grounds for belief proved (Howard Marine v Ogden); damages on fraud basis (Royscot)', 'innocent: reasonable grounds; rescission or damages in lieu under s2(2)'],
             apply=['the statement that it was {desc} was a false statement of fact that induced the sale', '{B} {belief}, so the misrepresentation is {belief_1}'],
             concl='the misrepresentation is {belief_1}')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the remedies available for misrepresentation.', ['rescission restores the parties', 's2(1) MA 1967 reverses the burden of proof, helping claimants', 'Royscot gives generous damages', 's2(2) gives flexibility'],
       ['Royscot fraud measure is criticised as illogical for negligent statements', 'bars to rescission can leave claimants without it', 'complex overlap with Hedley Byrne negligence'],
       ['Derry v Peek (1889)', 'Misrepresentation Act 1967 s2', 'Royscot Trust v Rogerson (1991)', 'Long v Lloyd (1958)', 'Leaf v International Galleries (1950)'], 'remedies are generally effective but the law is complex and the Royscot measure is hard to justify')])

T(3, 'Mistake: common, mutual and unilateral mistake', area='mistake',
  defs=[('common mistake', 'A mistake shared by both parties about a fundamental fact, such as the existence of the subject matter, which may make the contract void.'),
        ('mutual mistake', 'Where the parties misunderstand each other and are at cross-purposes, so there is no real agreement (Raffles v Wichelhaus).'),
        ('unilateral mistake', 'A mistake made by one party only, which the other party knows or ought to know about, often concerning identity or the terms of the contract.'),
        ('non est factum', '"It is not my deed": a narrow defence for a person who signs a document fundamentally different from what they believed, without carelessness (Saunders v Anglia Building Society).')],
  mcqs=[('Which case established that a contract is void if the subject matter no longer exists at the time of the contract?', 'Couturier v Hastie (1856)', ['Bell v Lever Bros (1932)', 'Raffles v Wichelhaus (1864)', 'Cundy v Lindsay (1878)'], 'The cargo of corn had already been sold.'),
        ('In Bell v Lever Bros (1932), the compensation agreement was not void because:', 'a mistake as to quality is not sufficiently fundamental', ['there was no consideration', 'it was a domestic agreement', 'the mistake was mutual'], 'Mistake about quality rarely makes a contract void.'),
        ('In Phillips v Brooks (1919), a rogue posing as Sir George Bullough bought a ring face to face. The contract was:', 'voidable for fraud, not void for mistake', ['void for mistake as to identity', 'valid and not voidable', 'illegal'], 'Face-to-face dealings: presumption of intention to deal with the person present (Lewis v Averay).'),
        ('Which case concerned a contract to buy cotton from a ship called "Peerless", when there were two ships of that name?', 'Raffles v Wichelhaus (1864)', ['Scriven v Hindley (1913)', 'Smith v Hughes (1871)', 'Hartog v Colin & Shields (1939)'], 'Mutual mistake: no agreement.'),
        ('In Shogun Finance v Hudson (2003), the contract with the rogue was void because:', 'it was a written contract and the identity of the named party was crucial', ['it was made face to face', 'the rogue was a minor', 'there was no consideration'], 'Written contracts: identity determined by the document.')],
  cases=[('Couturier v Hastie (1856)', 'a cargo of corn was sold when, unknown to both parties, it had already been sold by the ship\'s master because it was deteriorating', 'a contract for goods that no longer exist (res extincta) is void'),
         ('Bell v Lever Bros Ltd (1932)', 'a company paid compensation to two directors to end their contracts, not knowing they could have been dismissed without payment', 'a common mistake as to quality does not make the contract void unless it makes the subject matter essentially different'),
         ('Great Peace Shipping Ltd v Tsavliris Salvage (International) Ltd (2002)', 'a ship was hired to rescue a stricken vessel, both parties wrongly believing it was close by', 'common mistake makes a contract void only if performance is impossible or essentially different from what was agreed'),
         ('Raffles v Wichelhaus (1864)', 'a buyer and seller of cotton referred to different ships both called "Peerless"', 'where the parties are at cross-purposes and there is no objective agreement, there is no contract'),
         ('Smith v Hughes (1871)', 'a buyer bought oats believing them to be old oats, when they were new', 'a mistake about quality by one party, not induced by the other, does not affect the contract (objective test)'),
         ('Hartog v Colin and Shields (1939)', 'hare skins were mistakenly offered at a price per pound instead of per piece, which the buyer must have realised', 'a party cannot accept an offer he knows was made by mistake as to its terms'),
         ('Cundy v Lindsay (1878)', 'a rogue named Blenkarn ordered goods by letter, imitating the name of a respectable firm, Blenkiron & Co', 'in contracts made at a distance, a mistake as to identity can make the contract void'),
         ('Lewis v Averay (1972)', 'a rogue posing as a famous actor bought a car face to face with a worthless cheque', 'in face-to-face dealings there is a presumption that the seller intends to deal with the person present, so the contract is only voidable'),
         ('Shogun Finance Ltd v Hudson (2003)', 'a rogue used a stolen driving licence to obtain a car on hire purchase in another person\'s name', 'where a written contract names a party, the contract is with that named person, so the contract with the rogue was void'),
         ('Saunders v Anglia Building Society (1970)', 'an elderly widow signed a document without her glasses, believing it was a gift of her house to her nephew', 'non est factum is not available where the document is not fundamentally different or the signer was careless')],
  ars=[('A mistake as to quality rarely makes a contract void.', 'In Bell v Lever Bros (1932) a mistake about the directors\' contracts did not make the agreement void.', 'A'),
       ('In face-to-face dealings, a mistake as to identity usually makes the contract void.', 'Lewis v Averay (1972) presumed an intention to deal with the person present.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain the difference between common, mutual and unilateral mistake.', 'Common: both parties make the same fundamental mistake (Couturier v Hastie); mutual: they are at cross-purposes (Raffles v Wichelhaus); unilateral: one party is mistaken and the other knows or ought to know (Cundy v Lindsay; Hartog).', '1 mark each with example. [3]'),
        ('Explain why the courts distinguish between face-to-face dealings and contracts made at a distance when the mistake is about identity.', 'At a distance the identity of the named person is usually crucial, so the contract may be void (Cundy v Lindsay; Shogun Finance); face to face, the seller is presumed to intend to contract with the person present, so it is only voidable for fraud (Phillips v Brooks; Lewis v Averay), protecting innocent third parties.', '1 mark distance rule; 1 mark face-to-face rule; 1 mark third-party consequence. [3]')],
  cmps=[('a void contract', 'a voidable contract', ['a void contract has no legal effect from the start, a voidable contract is valid until rescinded', 'goods obtained under a void contract cannot pass good title to a third party, under a voidable contract they can if rescission has not occurred'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A} {how} a rogue, {B}, who claimed to be {fake}. {B} paid with a worthless cheque and sold the {item} to {C}, an innocent buyer.',
             slots={'item': ['car', 'motorbike', 'boat'],
                    'how': [('sold a {item} face to face at {A}\'s home to', 'face to face, so the contract was probably voidable, not void (Phillips v Brooks; Lewis v Averay)', 'can keep the car'), ('sold a {item} by email and a written finance agreement to', 'at a distance/in writing, so the contract was probably void (Cundy v Lindsay; Shogun Finance)', 'must return it to {A}')],
                    'fake': ['a famous footballer', 'a well-known local businessman', 'a respected doctor']},
             issue='unilateral mistake as to identity', wrong=['common mistake as to quality', 'misrepresentation as to opinion', 'frustration'],
             iq='Which vitiating factor is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {A} whether {A} can recover the {item} from {C}.',
             law=['a mistake as to identity may make a contract void if identity is crucial (Cundy v Lindsay)', 'face to face there is a presumption of an intention to deal with the person present, so the contract is voidable for fraud (Phillips v Brooks; Lewis v Averay)', 'written contracts identify the parties by the document (Shogun Finance v Hudson)', 'a third party in good faith acquires title under a voidable contract not yet rescinded'],
             apply=['{A} {how} {B}', 'this was {how_1}', 'so {C} {how_2}'],
             concl='{C} {how_2}'),
        dict(facts='{A} agreed to buy {what} from {B} for {p}. Unknown to both of them, {fact}.',
             slots={'what': ['a cargo of coffee on board a ship', 'a racehorse kept at a farm in {city}', 'a painting in a warehouse'], 'p': ['$40 000', '$15 000'],
                    'fact': [('it had been destroyed by fire the day before', 'res extincta, so the contract is void (Couturier v Hastie)'), ('it was worth much less than both believed', 'a mistake as to quality, which does not usually make the contract void (Bell v Lever Bros)')]},
             issue='common mistake', wrong=['mutual mistake', 'unilateral mistake as to identity', 'misrepresentation'],
             iq='Which vitiating factor is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {A} whether the contract is valid.',
             law=['common mistake as to the existence of the subject matter makes the contract void (Couturier v Hastie)', 'a mistake as to quality does not, unless the subject matter is essentially different (Bell v Lever Bros; Great Peace Shipping)'],
             apply=['both parties shared the mistake', 'the mistake was that {fact}, which is {fact_1}'],
             concl='the position is {fact_1}')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the law on mistake as to identity.', ['protects owners where identity is crucial (Cundy v Lindsay)', 'face-to-face presumption protects innocent third parties (Lewis v Averay)', 'Shogun Finance gives certainty for written contracts'],
       ['the distinction between face-to-face and distance dealings is arbitrary', 'one innocent party must lose', 'dissents in Shogun proposed a single rule of voidability'],
       ['Cundy v Lindsay (1878)', 'Phillips v Brooks (1919)', 'Ingram v Little (1961)', 'Lewis v Averay (1972)', 'Shogun Finance v Hudson (2003)'], 'the law is inconsistent; a rule that such contracts are voidable with loss-sharing would be fairer')])

T(3, 'Economic duress and undue influence', area='economic duress and undue influence',
  defs=[('economic duress', 'Illegitimate economic pressure that leaves the victim no practical choice but to agree to a contract or variation, making it voidable (DSND Subsea v Petroleum Geo-Services).'),
        ('actual undue influence', 'Where one party proves that the other used improper pressure or influence to obtain their agreement to a transaction (BCCI v Aboody).'),
        ('presumed undue influence', 'Where a relationship of trust and confidence exists and the transaction calls for explanation, undue influence is presumed unless rebutted, e.g. by independent advice.'),
        ('duress to the person', 'Threats of violence or unlawful detention used to force a person into a contract, which make it voidable (Barton v Armstrong).')],
  mcqs=[('In Atlas Express v Kafco (1989), the agreement to pay more was voidable because:', 'Kafco had no practical alternative but to agree to Atlas\'s demand', ['it was a domestic agreement', 'there was a common mistake', 'Atlas was a minor'], 'Economic duress.'),
        ('Which case set out the requirements for economic duress, including pressure that is illegitimate and a significant cause of the contract?', 'DSND Subsea v Petroleum Geo-Services (2000)', ['Williams v Roffey (1990)', 'Barton v Armstrong (1975)', 'Allcard v Skinner (1887)'], 'Dyson J\'s summary.'),
        ('In Royal Bank of Scotland v Etridge (No 2) (2001), a bank is "put on inquiry" when:', 'a wife stands surety for her husband\'s debts (a non-commercial guarantee)', ['any customer opens an account', 'a company borrows money', 'a husband guarantees his wife\'s business debts at arm\'s length only'], 'The bank must ensure she receives independent legal advice.'),
        ('What is the effect of economic duress on a contract?', 'It makes the contract voidable', ['It makes the contract void', 'It has no effect', 'It makes the contract illegal'], 'Rescission must be sought promptly (The Atlantic Baron).'),
        ('In Times Travel v Pakistan International Airlines (2021), the Supreme Court held that:', 'lawful act duress exists but requires reprehensible conduct, which was not shown', ['all hard bargaining is duress', 'economic duress no longer exists', 'only threats of violence count'], 'Using lawful commercial pressure is generally legitimate.')],
  cases=[('Barton v Armstrong (1975)', 'a company chairman agreed to buy shares after receiving death threats', 'a contract entered under threats of violence is voidable for duress to the person'),
         ('Atlas Express Ltd v Kafco (Importers and Distributors) Ltd (1989)', 'a carrier refused to deliver a small company\'s goods to a major retailer unless it agreed to a higher price', 'illegitimate economic pressure leaving no practical alternative is economic duress'),
         ('DSND Subsea Ltd v Petroleum Geo-Services ASA (2000)', 'a contractor threatened to stop work on an oil rig unless new terms were agreed', 'economic duress requires illegitimate pressure that is a significant cause of the contract and leaves no practical choice'),
         ('Pao On v Lau Yiu Long (1980)', 'a party threatened not to complete a share purchase unless a guarantee was given', 'commercial pressure alone is not duress; the victim\'s will must be coerced by illegitimate pressure'),
         ('North Ocean Shipping Co v Hyundai Construction (The Atlantic Baron) (1979)', 'a shipbuilder demanded 10% more under threat of not completing a ship, and the buyer paid without protest for eight months', 'a contract voidable for economic duress may be affirmed by delay'),
         ('Allcard v Skinner (1887)', 'a nun gave all her property to her religious order', 'gifts made in a relationship of trust and confidence may be set aside for presumed undue influence, but delay can bar the claim'),
         ('Royal Bank of Scotland v Etridge (No 2) (2001)', 'wives stood as sureties for their husbands\' business debts', 'banks are put on inquiry in non-commercial guarantees and must take steps to ensure the surety receives independent advice'),
         ('Barclays Bank plc v O\'Brien (1993)', 'a husband misrepresented the extent of a charge on the matrimonial home to his wife', 'a creditor may be affected by the debtor\'s undue influence or misrepresentation if it had notice'),
         ('Lloyds Bank Ltd v Bundy (1975)', 'an elderly farmer mortgaged his home to the bank to support his son\'s failing business, relying on the bank manager\'s advice', 'a relationship of trust with a bank manager can give rise to undue influence'),
         ('Bank of Credit and Commerce International SA v Aboody (1990)', 'a wife signed guarantees after her husband bullied her during a meeting', 'actual undue influence must be proved by the person alleging it')],
  ars=[('Commercial pressure is always economic duress.', 'Pao On v Lau Yiu Long distinguished legitimate commercial pressure from illegitimate pressure.', 'D'),
       ('Independent legal advice can rebut a presumption of undue influence.', 'The advice shows the transaction was the result of free and informed decision.', 'A')],
  exps=[('Explain the elements of economic duress.', 'Pressure that is illegitimate (e.g. a threat to break a contract); it must be a significant cause of the victim entering the contract; and the victim must have had no practical alternative (DSND Subsea; Atlas Express). Protest and prompt action help show duress (The Atlantic Baron).', '1 mark illegitimate pressure; 1 mark significant cause; 1 mark no practical choice; 1 mark case. [4]'),
        ('Explain the difference between actual and presumed undue influence.', 'Actual: the claimant proves that the other party exerted improper influence (BCCI v Aboody). Presumed: where there is a relationship of trust (e.g. solicitor-client, religious adviser) or one is proved, and the transaction calls for explanation, influence is presumed unless rebutted (Etridge).', '1 mark actual; 1 mark presumed; 1 mark relationships/rebuttal. [3]')],
  cmps=[('economic duress', 'undue influence', ['economic duress involves illegitimate threats, usually commercial; undue influence involves abuse of a relationship of trust', 'economic duress is a common law doctrine, undue influence is equitable', 'both make the contract voidable'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A} Ltd, a small {biz}, had a contract with {B} Ltd to deliver goods to a major retailer before {deadline}. Shortly before the deadline {B} Ltd said it would not deliver unless {A} Ltd agreed to pay {x} more. {A} Ltd could not find another carrier in time and agreed, {protest}.',
             slots={'biz': ['toy importer', 'clothing manufacturer', 'electronics firm'], 'deadline': ['Christmas', 'a major trade fair', 'the start of the school term'],
                    'x': ['50%', 'double the price', '$20 000'],
                    'protest': [('protesting in writing and refusing to pay as soon as the deliveries were complete', 'its protest and prompt action mean it can avoid the agreement to pay more'), ('saying nothing and paying the extra for the next year', 'its delay without protest probably affirmed the agreement, so it cannot now avoid it (The Atlantic Baron)')]},
             issue='economic duress', wrong=['undue influence', 'misrepresentation', 'practical benefit as consideration only'],
             iq='Which vitiating factor is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {A} Ltd whether it can avoid the agreement to pay more.',
             law=['economic duress: illegitimate pressure, a significant cause, no practical alternative (DSND Subsea; Atlas Express v Kafco)', 'a threat to break an existing contract is usually illegitimate', 'the contract is voidable but may be affirmed by delay (The Atlantic Baron)'],
             apply=['{B} Ltd threatened to break the existing contract', '{A} Ltd had no practical alternative before {deadline}', '{A} Ltd agreed, {protest}, so {protest_1}'],
             concl='{B} Ltd\'s threat was economic duress, but {protest_1}'),
        dict(facts='{A}, aged {age}, agreed to {deal} after advice from {B}, {rel}. {A} did not receive independent advice. The transaction was very much to {A}\'s disadvantage.',
             slots={'age': ['79', '84', '72'],
                    'deal': ['guarantee a large business loan for {B}\'s company', 'sell {A}\'s house to {B} at half its value', 'transfer all {A}\'s savings to {B}'],
                    'rel': ['{A}\'s carer, on whom {A} relied completely', '{A}\'s financial adviser', '{A}\'s nephew, who managed {A}\'s affairs']},
             issue='presumed undue influence', wrong=['economic duress', 'unilateral mistake', 'non est factum'],
             iq='Which vitiating factor is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {A} whether the transaction can be set aside.',
             law=['undue influence may be actual (BCCI v Aboody) or presumed where there is a relationship of trust and confidence and a transaction that calls for explanation (Etridge)', 'some relationships are presumed (e.g. solicitor and client); others must be proved (Lloyds Bank v Bundy)', 'the presumption can be rebutted, e.g. by independent advice', 'the remedy is rescission'],
             apply=['{B} is {rel}, which suggests trust and confidence', 'the transaction calls for explanation as it is very disadvantageous', 'no independent advice was given'],
             concl='undue influence is likely to be presumed, so the transaction can probably be set aside')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the development of economic duress.', ['protects weaker parties from abuse of bargaining power', 'requirements in DSND give structure', 'balances Williams v Roffey practical benefit'],
       ['line between legitimate and illegitimate pressure unclear', 'lawful act duress is narrow (Times Travel)', 'uncertainty for commercial parties'],
       ['Atlas Express v Kafco (1989)', 'DSND Subsea (2000)', 'The Atlantic Baron (1979)', 'Times Travel v PIA (2021)'], 'economic duress is a useful protection but its limits remain uncertain')])

T(3, 'Illegality and contracts in restraint of trade', area='illegality and restraint of trade',
  defs=[('restraint of trade clause', 'A term restricting a person\'s freedom to carry on their trade or profession, such as a clause in an employment contract or sale of a business; it is void unless reasonable.'),
        ('illegal contract', 'A contract that involves committing a crime or a civil wrong or is contrary to public policy, which the courts may refuse to enforce.'),
        ('severance', 'Removing the unreasonable part of a restraint clause so that the rest can be enforced, where this does not change the overall effect of the clause (Tillman v Egon Zehnder).')],
  mcqs=[('Which case established that a restraint of trade is valid only if reasonable between the parties and in the public interest?', 'Nordenfelt v Maxim Nordenfelt (1894)', ['Patel v Mirza (2016)', 'Mason v Provident Clothing (1913)', 'Fitch v Dewes (1921)'], 'A worldwide restraint on the seller of an arms business was upheld.'),
        ('In Patel v Mirza (2016), the Supreme Court held that the illegality defence should be decided by:', 'considering the purpose of the law broken, other relevant policies and proportionality', ['a strict reliance test', 'whether the claimant was a minor', 'the literal rule'], 'A flexible "range of factors" approach.'),
        ('Which interest can an employer legitimately protect with a restraint clause?', 'Trade secrets and customer connections', ['Preventing all competition', 'Keeping wages low', 'Preventing the employee from ever working again'], 'Herbert Morris v Saxelby (1916).'),
        ('In Fitch v Dewes (1921), a lifelong restraint on a solicitor\'s clerk working within 7 miles of Tamworth Town Hall was:', 'upheld as reasonable', ['void as too wide', 'illegal', 'unenforceable for lack of consideration'], 'The clerk had got to know the firm\'s clients.')],
  cases=[('Nordenfelt v Maxim Nordenfelt Guns and Ammunition Co (1894)', 'the seller of a worldwide arms business agreed not to compete anywhere in the world for 25 years', 'a restraint is valid if reasonable between the parties and not against the public interest; here it was upheld'),
         ('Mason v Provident Clothing and Supply Co (1913)', 'a canvasser was restrained from working within 25 miles of London for three years', 'a restraint wider than necessary to protect the employer\'s interests is void'),
         ('Fitch v Dewes (1921)', 'a solicitor\'s managing clerk was restrained for life from practising within seven miles of Tamworth', 'a restraint protecting client connections can be reasonable even if unlimited in time'),
         ('Forster & Sons Ltd v Suggett (1918)', 'a works manager who knew secret glass-making methods was restrained from working for competitors', 'restraints protecting trade secrets can be valid'),
         ('Esso Petroleum Co Ltd v Harper\'s Garage (Stourport) Ltd (1968)', 'a garage agreed to buy only Esso petrol, one tie for 4.5 years and one for 21 years', 'solus agreements are subject to the restraint doctrine; 4.5 years was reasonable but 21 years was not'),
         ('Patel v Mirza (2016)', 'a claimant paid money to the defendant to bet on shares using insider information, which never happened', 'the illegality defence depends on the purpose of the prohibition, other policies and proportionality, so the money could be recovered'),
         ('Tillman v Egon Zehnder Ltd (2019)', 'a non-compete clause prevented a recruitment consultant from being "interested" in a competing business', 'the unreasonable words could be severed, leaving the rest of the restraint enforceable')],
  ars=[('Restraint of trade clauses are prima facie void.', 'Public policy favours freedom to trade and to work.', 'A'),
       ('Restraints in the sale of a business are treated more strictly than in employment contracts.', 'Buyers of a business are entitled to protect the goodwill they paid for.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain how the courts decide whether a restraint of trade clause is reasonable.', 'There must be a legitimate interest to protect (trade secrets, customer connections, goodwill); the restraint must be reasonable in area, duration and activities (Mason v Provident; Fitch v Dewes); and not contrary to the public interest (Nordenfelt).', '1 mark legitimate interest; 1 mark area/time/scope; 1 mark public interest. [3]'),
        ('Explain the approach to illegality set out in Patel v Mirza (2016).', 'The court asks whether enforcing the claim would harm the integrity of the legal system, considering the purpose of the law that was broken, other relevant public policies, and whether denying the claim would be proportionate.', '1 mark each factor. [3]')],
  cmps=[('a restraint in an employment contract', 'a restraint on the seller of a business', ['employment restraints are treated more strictly because of unequal bargaining power', 'sale of business restraints protect goodwill paid for and may be wider (Nordenfelt)'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A} worked as a {job} for {B} Ltd in {city}. The contract said that after leaving, {A} would not {restr}. {A} has left and set up a competing business.',
             slots={'job': ['hairdresser', 'estate agent', 'software sales manager'],
                    'restr': [('work in the same trade within 1 km of the shop for six months', 'probably reasonable, as narrow in area and time to protect customer connections (Fitch v Dewes)'), ('work in the same trade anywhere in the country for ten years', 'probably void as wider than necessary (Mason v Provident)')]},
             issue='restraint of trade in an employment contract', wrong=['illegality under Patel v Mirza', 'economic duress', 'frustration'],
             iq='Which vitiating factor is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {B} Ltd whether it can enforce the restraint clause.',
             law=['restraints are prima facie void unless reasonable (Nordenfelt)', 'employer must have a legitimate interest such as trade secrets or customer connections (Forster v Suggett; Fitch v Dewes)', 'restraint must be reasonable in area, time and scope (Mason v Provident)', 'unreasonable parts may be severed (Tillman v Egon Zehnder)'],
             apply=['{B} Ltd has an interest in its customer connections', 'the restraint, which prevents {A} from agreeing to {restr}, is {restr_1}'],
             concl='the clause is {restr_1}')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the law on contracts in restraint of trade.', ['protects legitimate business interests', 'protects employees\' freedom to work', 'flexibility and severance (Tillman)'],
       ['uncertain outcomes for employers and employees', 'employees may comply with void clauses through fear', 'case-by-case approach is costly'],
       ['Nordenfelt (1894)', 'Mason v Provident (1913)', 'Fitch v Dewes (1921)', 'Esso v Harper\'s Garage (1968)', 'Tillman v Egon Zehnder (2019)'], 'the doctrine balances interests fairly but its uncertainty is a weakness')])

# =====================================================================================
# 4 Discharge and remedies
# =====================================================================================

T(4, 'Discharge by performance and the doctrine of substantial performance', area='discharge by performance',
  defs=[('discharge', 'The ending of contractual obligations, by performance, agreement, frustration or breach.'),
        ('entire contract', 'A contract under which complete performance is required before any payment is due (Cutter v Powell).'),
        ('substantial performance', 'A doctrine allowing a party who has performed almost all of the contract, with only minor defects, to claim the contract price less the cost of putting the defects right (Hoenig v Isaacs).'),
        ('quantum meruit', '"As much as he has earned": a claim for a reasonable sum for work done, e.g. where the other party prevented completion (Planché v Colburn).')],
  mcqs=[('In Cutter v Powell (1795), the widow recovered nothing because:', 'the contract was entire and her husband had not completed the voyage', ['the contract was frustrated', 'there was no consideration', 'the husband had breached a warranty'], 'Strict performance rule.'),
        ('In Hoenig v Isaacs (1952), the decorator could recover the price less the cost of repairs because:', 'he had substantially performed the contract', ['the contract was divisible', 'the customer prevented performance', 'the contract was frustrated'], 'Defects cost about £55 to fix out of £750.'),
        ('In Bolton v Mahadeva (1972), the heating installer recovered nothing because:', 'the defects were too serious to amount to substantial performance', ['the contract was illegal', 'he was prevented from finishing', 'the customer had accepted partial performance'], 'Fumes and inadequate heating; cost of repairs about a third of the price.'),
        ('Which case allowed a claim on a quantum meruit where the other party prevented completion?', 'Planché v Colburn (1831)', ['Sumpter v Hedges (1898)', 'Re Moore & Co and Landauer (1921)', 'Cutter v Powell (1795)'], 'The publisher abandoned the series of books.')],
  cases=[('Cutter v Powell (1795)', 'a sailor was to be paid 30 guineas at the end of a voyage but died before the ship arrived', 'under an entire contract nothing is payable unless performance is complete'),
         ('Re Moore & Co and Landauer & Co (1921)', 'tinned fruit was delivered in cases of 24 tins instead of 30, though the total number of tins was correct', 'performance must exactly match the contract terms, so the buyers could reject'),
         ('Sumpter v Hedges (1898)', 'a builder abandoned work on two houses and a stable when it was only partly finished', 'where work is voluntarily abandoned, there is no payment unless the other party freely accepts the partial performance'),
         ('Hoenig v Isaacs (1952)', 'a decorator furnished a flat for £750 with minor defects costing about £55 to fix', 'where a contract is substantially performed, the price is payable less the cost of remedying defects'),
         ('Bolton v Mahadeva (1972)', 'a central heating system was installed but gave off fumes and heated the house poorly', 'there is no substantial performance where defects are serious'),
         ('Planché v Colburn (1831)', 'an author was writing a book for a series that the publisher cancelled', 'where the other party prevents performance, the claimant can recover a reasonable sum on a quantum meruit'),
         ('Startup v Macdonald (1843)', 'a seller delivered oil at 8.30 pm on the last day of the period, and the buyer refused to accept it', 'a valid tender of performance that is refused discharges the party tendering')],
  ars=[('The rule of strict performance can be harsh.', 'In Cutter v Powell the widow received nothing although most of the voyage was completed.', 'A'),
       ('Substantial performance allows recovery of the full contract price with no deductions.', 'Under substantial performance the cost of remedying defects is deducted.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain three exceptions to the rule of strict performance.', 'Divisible contracts (payment for each part); substantial performance (Hoenig v Isaacs); acceptance of partial performance (Sumpter v Hedges); prevention of performance by the other party (Planché v Colburn); valid tender of performance (Startup v Macdonald).', '1 mark per exception with case, up to 3. [3]'),
        ('Explain the difference between Hoenig v Isaacs (1952) and Bolton v Mahadeva (1972).', 'In Hoenig the defects were minor compared with the price, so there was substantial performance; in Bolton the defects were serious and costly, so there was no substantial performance and nothing was recoverable.', '1 mark each case; 1 mark contrast based on seriousness of defects. [3]')],
  cmps=[('an entire contract', 'a divisible contract', ['an entire contract requires complete performance before payment, a divisible contract allows payment for each completed part'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A}, a builder, agreed to {job} for {B} for {p}, payable on completion. {A} {what}.',
             slots={'job': ['build an extension', 'install a new kitchen', 'refurbish a shop in {city}'], 'p': ['$30 000', '$18 000', '$50 000'],
                    'what': [('finished the work, but there were minor defects costing about $800 to put right', 'substantial performance, so {A} can claim the price less $800 (Hoenig v Isaacs)'),
                             ('left after completing about half the work because of a better offer elsewhere', 'abandonment, so {A} recovers nothing unless {B} freely accepted the part performance (Sumpter v Hedges)'),
                             ('was stopped halfway because {B} refused to let {A} back on site', 'prevention by {B}, so {A} can claim a quantum meruit for work done (Planché v Colburn)'),
                             ('finished the work but it was so defective that much of it had to be redone at a cost of a third of the price', 'no substantial performance, so {A} may recover nothing (Bolton v Mahadeva)')]},
             issue='discharge by performance and its exceptions', wrong=['frustration', 'misrepresentation', 'anticipatory breach'],
             ask='Advise {A} whether {A} can recover any payment from {B}.',
             law=['strict performance is required for entire contracts (Cutter v Powell; Re Moore & Landauer)', 'substantial performance: price less cost of defects (Hoenig v Isaacs) but not where defects are serious (Bolton v Mahadeva)', 'acceptance of part performance must be free (Sumpter v Hedges)', 'prevention allows quantum meruit (Planché v Colburn)'],
             apply=['the contract was entire, payable on completion', '{A} {what}', 'this is {what_1}'],
             concl='this is {what_1}')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the extent to which the exceptions to strict performance make the law fair.', ['substantial performance prevents unjust enrichment', 'quantum meruit protects those prevented from performing', 'divisible contracts allow payment for parts'],
       ['strict rule still applies harshly (Cutter v Powell; Bolton v Mahadeva)', 'acceptance must be free, which is hard where work is on land (Sumpter v Hedges)', 'uncertainty over what is "substantial"'],
       ['Cutter v Powell (1795)', 'Hoenig v Isaacs (1952)', 'Bolton v Mahadeva (1972)', 'Sumpter v Hedges (1898)', 'Planché v Colburn (1831)'], 'the exceptions greatly reduce the harshness of the rule, though not completely')])

T(4, 'Discharge by agreement, frustration and breach (actual and anticipatory)', area='discharge by frustration and breach',
  defs=[('frustration', 'The automatic discharge of a contract where, after it is made and without the fault of either party, an event makes performance impossible, illegal or radically different from what was agreed.'),
        ('anticipatory breach', 'Where one party indicates before the time for performance that they will not perform, allowing the other to sue immediately or wait for the performance date (Hochster v De la Tour).'),
        ('repudiatory breach', 'A breach of a condition or a serious breach of an innominate term that allows the innocent party to treat the contract as ended and claim damages.'),
        ('accord and satisfaction', 'Discharge by agreement where one party is released from obligations in return for new consideration.')],
  mcqs=[('Which case established the doctrine of frustration where the subject matter is destroyed?', 'Taylor v Caldwell (1863)', ['Krell v Henry (1903)', 'Davis Contractors v Fareham UDC (1956)', 'Hochster v De la Tour (1853)'], 'The music hall burned down before the concerts.'),
        ('In Davis Contractors v Fareham UDC (1956), the contract was not frustrated because:', 'performance had only become more expensive and took longer', ['the builders were at fault', 'the contract was illegal', 'there was a force majeure clause'], 'Mere hardship is not frustration.'),
        ('Under s1(2) Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943, money paid before frustration:', 'is recoverable, though the court may allow the payee to keep some to cover expenses', ['is never recoverable', 'must be paid twice', 'goes to the court'], 'Gamerco v ICM/Fair Warning (1995).'),
        ('In Maritime National Fish v Ocean Trawlers (1935), there was no frustration because:', 'the event was self-induced, as the charterers chose which boats to license', ['the ship sank', 'war broke out', 'the government banned fishing'], 'Self-induced frustration is not frustration.'),
        ('In Hochster v De la Tour (1853), the courier could sue before the tour was due to start because:', 'there was an anticipatory breach', ['the contract was frustrated', 'there was a misrepresentation', 'he had substantially performed'], 'He could sue immediately.')],
  cases=[('Taylor v Caldwell (1863)', 'a music hall hired for a series of concerts was destroyed by fire before the first concert', 'a contract is frustrated if the subject matter is destroyed without the fault of either party'),
         ('Krell v Henry (1903)', 'a flat was hired to watch the coronation procession of Edward VII, which was cancelled because of the King\'s illness', 'a contract is frustrated if its sole purpose can no longer be achieved'),
         ('Herne Bay Steam Boat Co v Hutton (1903)', 'a boat was hired to see the naval review and cruise round the fleet, but the review was cancelled', 'a contract is not frustrated if part of its purpose can still be achieved'),
         ('Condor v The Baron Knights (1966)', 'a drummer became ill and could perform only four nights a week instead of seven', 'personal incapacity can frustrate a contract for personal services'),
         ('Jackson v Union Marine Insurance (1874)', 'a ship ran aground and was delayed for eight months', 'a long delay that defeats the commercial purpose can frustrate a contract'),
         ('Davis Contractors Ltd v Fareham UDC (1956)', 'a builder took 22 months instead of 8 to build houses because of labour shortages, and costs rose', 'a contract is not frustrated merely because it becomes more expensive or difficult'),
         ('Maritime National Fish Ltd v Ocean Trawlers Ltd (1935)', 'charterers given only three fishing licences chose not to allocate one to the chartered trawler', 'self-induced frustration does not discharge the contract'),
         ('Hochster v De la Tour (1853)', 'an employer told a courier before the start date that his services would not be needed', 'an anticipatory breach allows the innocent party to sue immediately'),
         ('White and Carter (Councils) Ltd v McGregor (1962)', 'a company continued to display adverts after the customer cancelled, and claimed the full price', 'after anticipatory breach the innocent party may affirm and perform if it has a legitimate interest and can perform without the other\'s cooperation'),
         ('Avery v Bowden (1855)', 'a charterer repudiated a contract, the owner waited, and the Crimean War then frustrated it', 'if the innocent party waits after anticipatory breach, it risks the contract being frustrated'),
         ('Tsakiroglou & Co Ltd v Noblee Thorl GmbH (1962)', 'the closure of the Suez Canal meant goods had to be shipped the longer way round the Cape of Good Hope', 'a longer and more expensive route does not frustrate the contract')],
  ars=[('A contract does not become frustrated just because it becomes more expensive to perform.', 'Frustration requires performance to be impossible or radically different.', 'A'),
       ('Frustration must be caused by one of the parties.', 'Self-induced frustration does not discharge a contract.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain three situations in which a contract may be frustrated.', 'Destruction of the subject matter (Taylor v Caldwell); non-occurrence of the event that is the purpose of the contract (Krell v Henry); personal incapacity (Condor v Baron Knights); supervening illegality; government interference; long delay (Jackson v Union Marine).', '1 mark per situation with case, up to 3. [3]'),
        ('Explain the effect of the Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943.', 'Money paid before the frustrating event is recoverable and money due ceases to be payable (s1(2)); the court may allow the payee to retain or recover expenses; a party who has obtained a valuable benefit may have to pay a just sum (s1(3); BP v Hunt).', '1 mark s1(2) recovery; 1 mark expenses; 1 mark s1(3) valuable benefit. [3]'),
        ('Explain the options available to the innocent party after an anticipatory breach.', 'They may accept the repudiation and sue immediately (Hochster v De la Tour), or affirm the contract and wait for the date of performance, taking the risk of later frustration (Avery v Bowden) or performing if they have a legitimate interest (White and Carter v McGregor).', '1 mark accept and sue; 1 mark affirm and wait; 1 mark risks/case. [3]')],
  cmps=[('Krell v Henry (1903)', 'Herne Bay Steam Boat v Hutton (1903)', ['in Krell the sole purpose (watching the procession) was defeated, in Herne Bay part of the purpose (cruising round the fleet) remained', 'so Krell was frustrated and Herne Bay was not'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A} hired {what} from {B} for {date} for {purpose}, paying a deposit of {dep} with the balance of {bal} due on the day. {event}',
             slots={'what': ['a hall in {city}', 'a riverside venue', 'a marquee and stage'], 'date': ['15 June', '3 August', 'New Year\'s Eve'],
                    'purpose': ['a wedding reception', 'a music festival', 'a company celebration'], 'dep': ['$2 000', '$5 000'], 'bal': ['$8 000', '$15 000'],
                    'event': [('Two days before, the venue was destroyed by fire without anyone\'s fault.', 'frustrated by destruction of the subject matter (Taylor v Caldwell)'),
                              ('A week before, the cost of catering rose sharply and {A} said the event was no longer worth holding.', 'not frustrated, as increased cost is not enough (Davis Contractors v Fareham)'),
                              ('A month before, the government banned all public gatherings in the area for the date.', 'frustrated by supervening illegality')]},
             issue='frustration and the Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943', wrong=['anticipatory breach only', 'common mistake', 'misrepresentation'],
             ask='Advise {A} whether the contract has been discharged and whether the deposit can be recovered.',
             law=['frustration occurs where, without fault, performance becomes impossible, illegal or radically different (Taylor v Caldwell; Krell v Henry)', 'increased cost or difficulty is not enough (Davis Contractors); self-induced events do not count (Maritime National Fish)', 'LR(FC)A 1943 s1(2): money paid is recoverable and money due ceases to be payable, subject to the court allowing expenses'],
             apply=['the event means the contract is {event_1}', 'if frustrated, {A} can recover the {dep} deposit and need not pay the {bal}, subject to a deduction for {B}\'s expenses', 'if not frustrated, {A}\'s refusal is a breach'],
             concl='the contract is {event_1}'),
        dict(facts='On 1 March {A} agreed to work as a {role} for {B}\'s {biz} from 1 June. On 10 April {B} told {A}, "We will not need you after all."',
             slots={'role': ['tour guide', 'chef', 'sound engineer'], 'biz': ['travel company', 'restaurant', 'concert promoter']},
             issue='anticipatory breach', wrong=['frustration', 'discharge by agreement', 'substantial performance'],
             ask='Advise {A} on the options available.',
             law=['anticipatory breach: a party indicates before performance is due that it will not perform (Hochster v De la Tour)', 'the innocent party may accept the repudiation and sue at once, or affirm and wait', 'waiting risks the contract being frustrated (Avery v Bowden); performance after affirming requires a legitimate interest (White and Carter v McGregor)', '{A} must mitigate loss by seeking other work'],
             apply=['{B}\'s statement on 10 April is an anticipatory breach', '{A} can sue immediately without waiting for 1 June', '{A} should look for other work to mitigate'],
             concl='{A} can accept the repudiation and claim damages immediately, subject to the duty to mitigate')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the doctrine of frustration.', ['relieves parties from obligations made impossible by events beyond their control', 'narrow limits preserve the sanctity of contract (Davis Contractors)', '1943 Act provides flexible financial adjustment'],
       ['outcomes depend on fine distinctions (Krell v Henry v Herne Bay)', 'the 1943 Act gives wide discretion and uncertainty', 'parties often prefer force majeure clauses'],
       ['Taylor v Caldwell (1863)', 'Krell v Henry (1903)', 'Herne Bay v Hutton (1903)', 'Davis Contractors (1956)', 'Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943'], 'the doctrine is a sensible narrow safety valve, with the 1943 Act softening the consequences')])

t = T(4, 'Damages: causation, remoteness and mitigation of loss', area='damages for breach of contract',
      defs=[('expectation loss', 'Damages that put the claimant in the position they would have been in if the contract had been performed (Robinson v Harman).'),
            ('reliance loss', 'Damages for expenses wasted in reliance on the contract, putting the claimant in the position they were in before the contract (Anglia Television v Reed).'),
            ('remoteness of damage', 'The limit on recoverable loss: only losses arising naturally from the breach, or within the reasonable contemplation of both parties when the contract was made, are recoverable (Hadley v Baxendale).'),
            ('mitigation of loss', 'The duty of the claimant to take reasonable steps to reduce the loss caused by the breach; loss that could have been avoided is not recoverable.'),
            ('liquidated damages', 'A sum agreed in the contract as payable on breach, enforceable unless it is a penalty out of all proportion to the innocent party\'s legitimate interest (Cavendish v Makdessi; ParkingEye v Beavis).')],
      mcqs=[('Which case set out the two limbs of the test for remoteness in contract?', 'Hadley v Baxendale (1854)', ['Robinson v Harman (1848)', 'The Wagon Mound (No 1) (1961)', 'Jarvis v Swans Tours (1973)'], 'Losses arising naturally, or in the reasonable contemplation of both parties.'),
            ('In Victoria Laundry v Newman (1949), the claimant could not recover the profits on especially lucrative dyeing contracts because:', 'the defendants did not know about those contracts, so the loss was too remote', ['the laundry failed to mitigate', 'the contract was frustrated', 'damages for lost profits are never recoverable'], 'Ordinary lost profits were recoverable.'),
            ('In which case were damages awarded for disappointment and distress caused by a ruined holiday?', 'Jarvis v Swans Tours (1973)', ['Addis v Gramophone (1909)', 'Hadley v Baxendale (1854)', 'British Westinghouse v Underground Electric Railways (1912)'], 'Exception for contracts whose purpose is pleasure or peace of mind.'),
            ('In Ruxley Electronics v Forsyth (1995), the swimming pool was 18 inches too shallow at the deep end. The House of Lords awarded:', 'damages for loss of amenity rather than the full cost of rebuilding the pool', ['the full cost of rebuilding', 'nothing at all', 'specific performance'], 'Cost of cure was unreasonable compared with the benefit.'),
            ('The modern test for whether a liquidated damages clause is a penalty was set out in:', 'Cavendish Square Holding v Makdessi; ParkingEye v Beavis (2015)', ['Dunlop v New Garage only (1915)', 'Hadley v Baxendale (1854)', 'Hong Kong Fir (1962)'], 'Legitimate interest and proportionality.')],
      cases=[('Robinson v Harman (1848)', 'a landlord broke a promise to grant a valid lease', 'damages aim to put the claimant in the position they would have been in had the contract been performed'),
             ('Anglia Television Ltd v Reed (1972)', 'an actor pulled out of a television film after the company had spent money on preparations', 'a claimant may recover wasted expenditure (reliance loss), including expenses before the contract'),
             ('Hadley v Baxendale (1854)', 'a mill was closed longer than expected because a carrier delayed returning a broken crankshaft, and the mill had no spare', 'recoverable loss is that arising naturally from the breach or within the reasonable contemplation of both parties at the time of contracting'),
             ('Victoria Laundry (Windsor) Ltd v Newman Industries Ltd (1949)', 'a boiler was delivered five months late, causing loss of normal profits and of very lucrative government contracts', 'normal lost profits are recoverable but unusual losses unknown to the defendant are too remote'),
             ('Koufos v C Czarnikow Ltd (The Heron II) (1969)', 'a ship deviated, delivering sugar late into a falling market', 'loss is recoverable if it was "not unlikely" to result from the breach, a higher degree of probability than in tort'),
             ('Transfield Shipping Inc v Mercator Shipping Inc (The Achilleas) (2008)', 'late return of a chartered ship caused the owners to lose a very profitable next charter', 'a party is liable only for losses of a type for which it can be taken to have assumed responsibility'),
             ('British Westinghouse Electric Co v Underground Electric Railways Co (1912)', 'defective turbines were replaced by better ones that saved the buyers more money than the loss', 'the claimant must mitigate, and benefits gained from mitigation are taken into account'),
             ('Ruxley Electronics and Construction Ltd v Forsyth (1995)', 'a swimming pool was built 18 inches shallower than agreed at the deep end, but was perfectly safe', 'where cost of cure is out of all proportion to the benefit, damages may be for loss of amenity'),
             ('Jarvis v Swans Tours Ltd (1973)', 'a solicitor\'s skiing holiday did not match the brochure\'s promises', 'damages for mental distress can be awarded where the contract is for pleasure or enjoyment'),
             ('Farley v Skinner (2001)', 'a surveyor negligently reported that a house was not affected by aircraft noise', 'damages for distress are available where an important object of the contract was peace of mind or pleasure'),
             ('Addis v Gramophone Co Ltd (1909)', 'a manager was dismissed in a humiliating way', 'damages are not generally awarded for injured feelings in breach of contract'),
             ('Cavendish Square Holding BV v Makdessi; ParkingEye Ltd v Beavis (2015)', 'a car park charged £85 for overstaying a two-hour free limit', 'a clause is a penalty only if it imposes a detriment out of all proportion to the innocent party\'s legitimate interest in performance')],
      ars=[('A claimant cannot recover loss that could reasonably have been avoided.', 'The claimant has a duty to mitigate loss.', 'A'),
           ('Damages for mental distress are always available in contract.', 'Addis v Gramophone (1909) held that injured feelings are generally not compensated.', 'D')],
      exps=[('Explain the two limbs of the rule in Hadley v Baxendale (1854).', 'Limb 1: losses arising naturally, in the usual course of things, from the breach; limb 2: losses that both parties may reasonably be supposed to have contemplated at the time of the contract as a probable result of breach, e.g. because of special knowledge (Victoria Laundry).', '1 mark limb 1; 1 mark limb 2; 1 mark example/case. [3]'),
            ('Explain the difference between expectation and reliance damages.', 'Expectation damages put the claimant in the position as if the contract had been performed (Robinson v Harman), e.g. loss of profit; reliance damages compensate wasted expenditure, putting the claimant back where they were (Anglia TV v Reed), used where profits are speculative.', '1 mark each with explanation; 1 mark when reliance is used. [3]'),
            ('Explain the duty to mitigate loss.', 'The claimant must take reasonable steps to reduce the loss, e.g. buying substitute goods or seeking other work; loss that could have been avoided is not recoverable, but reasonable costs of mitigation are; the burden is on the defendant to show failure (British Westinghouse).', '1 mark duty; 1 mark consequence; 1 mark example/case. [3]')],
      cmps=[('remoteness in contract', 'remoteness in tort', ['in contract loss must be within the reasonable contemplation of the parties (Hadley v Baxendale; The Heron II), in tort it must be a reasonably foreseeable type (The Wagon Mound)', 'the contract test requires a higher degree of probability'])],
      scen=[dict(facts='{B} Ltd agreed to repair a {mach} for {A} Ltd, a {biz} in {city}, within three days. The repair took {late} longer than agreed. {A} Ltd lost normal profits of {norm} and also lost a special contract worth {special}, which {knew}.',
                 slots={'mach': ['bakery oven', 'printing press', 'refrigeration unit'], 'biz': ['bakery', 'printing firm', 'seafood exporter'],
                        'late': ['two weeks', 'three weeks'], 'norm': ['$6 000', '$9 000'], 'special': ['$40 000', '$75 000'],
                        'knew': [('{B} Ltd had been told about when the contract was made', 'recoverable under the second limb because {B} Ltd knew of it'), ('{B} Ltd knew nothing about', 'too remote, as in Victoria Laundry')]},
                 issue='remoteness of damage in contract', wrong=['mitigation by buying substitute goods', 'frustration', 'liquidated damages'],
                 ask='Advise {A} Ltd on the damages it can recover from {B} Ltd.',
                 law=['remoteness: losses arising naturally, or in the contemplation of both parties (Hadley v Baxendale)', 'damages put the claimant in the position as if the contract had been performed (Robinson v Harman)', 'unusual profits unknown to the defendant are too remote (Victoria Laundry); assumption of responsibility (The Achilleas)', 'the claimant must mitigate'],
                 apply=['the normal lost profits of {norm} arise naturally from the delay (limb 1)', 'the special contract worth {special} is {knew_1}', '{A} Ltd should have tried to hire a replacement {mach} to mitigate'],
                 concl='{A} Ltd can recover {norm} in normal lost profits; the {special} special contract is {knew_1}')])


@t.add('C3', (1, 2, 3, 4, 5), 'numerical')
def damages_calc(r, D):
    A, B = r.sample(PEOPLE, 2)
    goods = r.choice([('tonnes of coffee beans', 50, 400), ('laptops', 20, 600), ('bags of cement', 500, 8), ('tonnes of steel', 40, 700)])
    n = r.choice([10, 20, 25, 40, 50, 100]) * (10 if goods[2] < 20 else 1)
    p = goods[2] * r.choice([1, 1.1, 1.2, 0.9])
    p = round(p)
    m = round(p * r.choice([1.1, 1.15, 1.2, 1.25, 1.3]))
    loss = (m - p) * n
    if D == 1:
        opts, ans = money_mcq(r, loss, [m * n, p * n, (m + p) * n / 2])
        return {'stem': f'{B} Ltd agreed to sell {n} {goods[0]} to {A} Ltd at {money(p)} each but failed to deliver. {A} Ltd bought the same goods elsewhere at the market price of {money(m)} each. What are {A} Ltd\'s expectation damages?',
                'options': opts, 'answer': ans, 'key': f'Market price minus contract price × quantity = ({money(m)} − {money(p)}) × {n} = {money(loss)}.', 'marks': 1}
    stem = (f'{B} Ltd agreed to sell {n} {goods[0]} to {A} Ltd at {money(p)} each, for delivery on 1 May. {B} Ltd failed to deliver. '
            f'On 1 May the market price was {money(m)} each.')
    key = f'Expectation damages = (market price − contract price) × quantity = ({money(m)} − {money(p)}) × {n} = {money(loss)} [2]'
    ans = money(loss)
    mk = 2
    if D >= 3:
        m2 = round(m * r.choice([1.1, 1.2, 1.25]))
        stem += f' {A} Ltd waited a month and then bought replacement goods at {money(m2)} each.'
        key = (f'Damages are based on the market price at the date of breach because {A} Ltd should have mitigated by buying promptly [1]; '
               f'({money(m)} − {money(p)}) × {n} = {money(loss)} [2]; the extra ({money(m2)} − {money(m)}) × {n} = {money((m2 - m) * n)} is not recoverable [1]')
        ans = f'{money(loss)}; the further {money((m2 - m) * n)} is not recoverable because of the failure to mitigate.'
        mk = 4
    if D >= 4:
        lp = r.choice([3000, 5000, 8000, 12000])
        stem += f' {A} Ltd also says it lost a resale contract which would have earned a profit of {money(lp)}; {B} Ltd did not know about this contract.'
        key += f'; the {money(lp)} lost resale profit is too remote under Hadley v Baxendale / Victoria Laundry as {B} Ltd did not know of it [2]'
        ans += f' The {money(lp)} lost profit is too remote.'
        mk = 6
    stem += ' Calculate the damages ' + ('and explain your answer.' if D >= 3 else f'{A} Ltd can recover.')
    return {'stem': stem, 'answer': ans, 'key': key + f'. [{mk}]', 'type': 'numerical', 'marks': mk}


T(4, 'Equitable remedies: specific performance, injunction and rescission', area='equitable remedies',
  defs=[('specific performance', 'An equitable order requiring a party to carry out their contractual obligations, granted only where damages would be inadequate, e.g. for land or unique goods.'),
        ('injunction', 'An equitable order requiring a party to do (mandatory) or not to do (prohibitory) something, such as not breaking a negative stipulation in a contract.'),
        ('equitable maxims', 'Principles guiding the discretion to grant equitable remedies, such as "he who comes to equity must come with clean hands" and "delay defeats equity".'),
        ('rectification', 'An equitable remedy correcting a written document so that it reflects what the parties actually agreed.')],
  mcqs=[('Specific performance is most likely to be ordered in a contract for:', 'the sale of land', ['personal services', 'ordinary goods readily available elsewhere', 'a contract requiring constant supervision'], 'Land is unique, so damages are inadequate.'),
        ('In Co-operative Insurance v Argyll Stores (1998), specific performance of a "keep open" covenant was refused because:', 'it would require constant supervision by the court', ['the contract was void', 'damages had already been paid', 'the tenants had clean hands'], 'Courts avoid orders needing continuous supervision.'),
        ('In Warner Bros v Nelson (1937), the court:', 'granted an injunction stopping Bette Davis working as an actress for another studio', ['ordered her to act for Warner Bros', 'refused any remedy', 'awarded damages only'], 'An injunction to enforce a negative stipulation.'),
        ('Which maxim was applied in D & C Builders v Rees (1966)?', '"He who comes to equity must come with clean hands"', ['"Delay defeats equity"', '"Equity follows the law"', '"Equity looks to substance not form"'], 'The debtor\'s wife had taken advantage of the builders\' financial difficulty.')],
  cases=[('Beswick v Beswick (1967)', 'a nephew refused to pay the agreed annuity to his uncle\'s widow', 'specific performance can be ordered where damages would be inadequate'),
         ('Falcke v Gray (1859)', 'a buyer sought to enforce a contract for two rare china vases', 'specific performance may be granted for unique goods'),
         ('Ryan v Mutual Tontine Westminster Chambers Association (1893)', 'a landlord agreed to provide a resident porter who was constantly absent', 'specific performance will not be ordered where it requires constant supervision'),
         ('Co-operative Insurance Society Ltd v Argyll Stores (Holdings) Ltd (1998)', 'a supermarket closed in breach of a covenant to keep the store open', 'specific performance of an obligation to run a business will normally be refused'),
         ('Lumley v Wagner (1852)', 'an opera singer agreed to sing only at Lumley\'s theatre for three months but agreed to sing elsewhere', 'an injunction can enforce a negative promise in a contract for personal services'),
         ('Warner Bros Pictures Inc v Nelson (1937)', 'Bette Davis agreed not to act for any other film company and then broke the agreement', 'an injunction may restrain breach of a negative stipulation if it does not compel performance'),
         ('Page One Records Ltd v Britton (1968)', 'a pop group wanted to dismiss its manager in breach of contract', 'an injunction will be refused if it would in effect compel performance of personal services'),
         ('D & C Builders Ltd v Rees (1966)', 'a customer pressured struggling builders into accepting a smaller sum in settlement', 'a party who has acted inequitably cannot rely on equity')],
  ars=[('Specific performance is a discretionary remedy.', 'Equitable remedies are granted only where it is just and equitable to do so.', 'A'),
       ('Courts regularly order specific performance of contracts for personal services.', 'Courts will not compel someone to work for another person.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain when specific performance will not be granted.', 'Where damages are adequate; for personal services; where constant supervision would be needed (Ryan; Co-op v Argyll); where the claimant has acted unfairly (clean hands) or delayed; where it would cause severe hardship; where there is no mutuality.', '1 mark per limitation, up to 3. [3]'),
        ('Explain how injunctions can be used in contract law.', 'A prohibitory injunction can restrain a breach of a negative stipulation, e.g. not to work for a competitor (Lumley v Wagner; Warner Bros v Nelson), but not if it would effectively compel performance of personal services (Page One Records v Britton).', '1 mark prohibitory injunction; 1 mark negative stipulation case; 1 mark limitation. [3]')],
  cmps=[('damages', 'specific performance', ['damages are a common law remedy available as of right, specific performance is equitable and discretionary', 'damages compensate in money, specific performance compels the party to perform'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{B} agreed to sell {A} {what} for {p}. Before completion, {B} received a higher offer and refused to go ahead. {A} {delay}.',
             slots={'what': [('a house in {city}', 'land is unique, so damages are inadequate and specific performance is likely', '$250 000'), ('a rare vintage car', 'unique goods, so specific performance may be ordered (Falcke v Gray)', '$40 000'), ('a standard new laptop', 'ordinary goods available elsewhere, so damages are adequate and specific performance is unlikely', '$1 500')],
                    'p': ['{what_2}'],
                    'delay': ['started legal proceedings immediately', 'waited two years before taking any action']},
             issue='specific performance as an equitable remedy', wrong=['rescission for misrepresentation', 'frustration', 'liquidated damages'],
             ask='Advise {A} whether a court is likely to order specific performance.',
             law=['specific performance is discretionary and granted only where damages are inadequate', 'land and unique goods are typical examples (Falcke v Gray)', 'it is refused for personal services or where constant supervision is needed (Co-op v Argyll)', 'equitable maxims apply: clean hands; delay defeats equity'],
             apply=['the contract was for {what}: {what_1}', '{A} {delay}, which is relevant because delay defeats equity'],
             concl='{what_1}, subject to {A} acting promptly')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the role of equitable remedies in contract law.', ['provide justice where damages are inadequate (land, unique goods)', 'flexible and discretionary', 'injunctions enforce negative promises'],
       ['discretion causes uncertainty', 'limits on personal services and supervision', 'cost and delay of seeking equitable relief'],
       ['Beswick v Beswick (1967)', 'Co-op v Argyll (1998)', 'Lumley v Wagner (1852)', 'Warner Bros v Nelson (1937)', 'Page One Records v Britton (1968)'], 'equitable remedies fill important gaps, but their discretionary nature limits their reach')])

# =====================================================================================
# 5 Negligence
# =====================================================================================

T(5, 'Duty of care: the Caparo test and incremental approach', area='duty of care',
  defs=[('negligence', 'A tort where the defendant owes the claimant a duty of care, breaches that duty and causes the claimant damage that is not too remote.'),
        ('duty of care', 'A legal obligation to take reasonable care to avoid causing foreseeable harm to others, established by precedent or by the Caparo/incremental approach.'),
        ('neighbour principle', 'Lord Atkin\'s test in Donoghue v Stevenson: you must take reasonable care to avoid acts or omissions which you can reasonably foresee would be likely to injure persons so closely and directly affected that you ought to have them in contemplation.'),
        ('proximity', 'A relationship of closeness between claimant and defendant, in terms of time, space or relationship, required for a duty of care.'),
        ('incremental approach', 'The approach confirmed in Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire (2018): courts apply established duty categories and develop new ones by analogy with existing cases, using Caparo factors only in novel situations.')],
  mcqs=[('What are the three stages of the Caparo v Dickman (1990) test?', 'Reasonable foreseeability of harm, proximity, and whether it is fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty', ['Duty, breach and damage', 'Offer, acceptance and consideration', 'But for, remoteness and mitigation'], 'Caparo Industries plc v Dickman.'),
        ('In Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire (2018), the Supreme Court held that:', 'the Caparo test is not needed where a duty is already established by precedent', ['the police can never owe a duty of care', 'Donoghue v Stevenson was overruled', 'duty of care is a question of fact for juries'], 'Police officers making an arrest owed a duty to a passer-by they knocked over.'),
        ('Which case decided that the police did not owe a duty of care to a potential victim of a criminal they had failed to catch?', 'Hill v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire (1989)', ['Kent v Griffiths (2000)', 'Bourhill v Young (1943)', 'Robinson v CC West Yorkshire (2018)'], 'Lack of proximity and policy reasons.'),
        ('In Kent v Griffiths (2000), the ambulance service owed a duty of care because:', 'it had accepted the call and the patient was a specific, identified person', ['all public services owe duties to everyone', 'the patient had paid for the service', 'there was a contract'], 'Proximity once the call was accepted.')],
  cases=[('Donoghue v Stevenson (1932)', 'a woman became ill after drinking ginger beer containing the decomposed remains of a snail, bought for her by a friend', 'a manufacturer owes a duty of care to the ultimate consumer, and Lord Atkin\'s neighbour principle is the basis of the modern duty of care'),
         ('Caparo Industries plc v Dickman (1990)', 'investors bought shares relying on negligently audited accounts', 'in novel cases a duty requires foreseeability, proximity and that it is fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty'),
         ('Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire Police (2018)', 'an elderly woman was knocked over when police officers arrested a suspect in the street', 'where a duty is established by precedent the court applies it; Caparo is only used for genuinely novel situations'),
         ('Bourhill v Young (1943)', 'a pregnant fishwife heard a motorcycle accident some distance away and suffered shock and a miscarriage', 'a duty is owed only to those who are reasonably foreseeable as likely to be harmed'),
         ('Hill v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire (1989)', 'the mother of the last victim of the "Yorkshire Ripper" sued the police for failing to catch him earlier', 'the police owe no general duty to individual members of the public in investigating crime'),
         ('Kent v Griffiths (2000)', 'an ambulance took 40 minutes to arrive for a patient having an asthma attack, without good reason', 'an ambulance service that accepts a call owes a duty to the patient'),
         ('Michael v Chief Constable of South Wales Police (2015)', 'police failed to respond quickly to a 999 call and the caller was murdered by her ex-partner', 'there is generally no duty to protect a person from harm caused by a third party unless the defendant assumed responsibility or created the danger')],
  ars=[('A new duty of care should be developed incrementally by analogy with established cases.', 'In Robinson v CC West Yorkshire (2018) the Supreme Court confirmed the incremental approach.', 'A'),
       ('The police owe a general duty of care to all members of the public to prevent crime.', 'Hill v CC West Yorkshire (1989) found no duty to a potential victim.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain the three stages of the Caparo test.', 'Was damage reasonably foreseeable (Bourhill v Young)? Was there a relationship of proximity between the parties? Is it fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty, considering policy factors such as floodgates and defensive practice (Hill v CC West Yorkshire)?', '1 mark per stage, with explanation/case. [3]'),
        ('Explain the significance of Donoghue v Stevenson (1932).', 'It established that a manufacturer owes a duty to the ultimate consumer without a contract, and created the neighbour principle as a general basis for duty of care, founding the modern tort of negligence.', '1 mark facts; 1 mark manufacturer\'s duty; 1 mark neighbour principle/general significance. [3]'),
        ('Explain why the courts are cautious about imposing a duty of care on public bodies such as the police.', 'Policy reasons: imposing a duty might lead to defensive practices, divert resources from their main tasks and open the floodgates of claims (Hill v CC West Yorkshire); there is generally no duty to protect people from third parties (Michael), though there is a duty not to cause positive harm (Robinson).', '1 mark per policy reason, up to 2. [2]')],
  cmps=[('the neighbour principle', 'the Caparo test', ['the neighbour principle is a broad test based on foreseeability and proximity, Caparo adds a third stage of fair, just and reasonable', 'Caparo is used for novel cases, reflecting a more cautious, incremental approach'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A} {situation}. {B} was injured as a result. {B} wants to sue {A} in negligence.',
             slots={'situation': [('drove carelessly through a junction in {city} and hit {B}, a pedestrian', 'an established duty: road users owe a duty to other road users (no need for Caparo, Robinson)'),
                                  ('manufactured a hair dye that caused {B}, a customer of a salon, to suffer burns', 'an established duty of manufacturers to consumers (Donoghue v Stevenson)'),
                                  ('ran a gym where an unsecured weight fell on {B}, a member', 'an established or analogous duty to visitors and customers'),
                                  ('is a police officer who failed to catch a burglar who later broke into {B}\'s house', 'a novel/excluded category: generally no duty to protect from third parties (Hill; Michael)')]},
             issue='whether a duty of care is owed', wrong=['breach of duty and the reasonable person', 'remoteness of damage', 'vicarious liability'],
             ask='Advise {B} whether {A} owed {B} a duty of care.',
             law=['established duties are applied by precedent (Robinson v CC West Yorkshire 2018)', 'novel cases: Caparo test of foreseeability, proximity and fair, just and reasonable, developed incrementally', 'neighbour principle (Donoghue v Stevenson)', 'no general duty to prevent harm by third parties (Hill; Michael)'],
             apply=['{A} {situation}', 'this is {situation_1}'],
             concl='this is {situation_1}')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the approach of the courts to establishing a duty of care in negligence.', ['incremental approach gives certainty and prevents floodgates', 'Caparo allows policy to be considered openly', 'Robinson clarified the law'],
       ['"fair, just and reasonable" is vague and allows judicial policy-making', 'public bodies may escape responsibility', 'inconsistent outcomes'],
       ['Donoghue v Stevenson (1932)', 'Caparo v Dickman (1990)', 'Hill v CC West Yorkshire (1989)', 'Robinson v CC West Yorkshire (2018)', 'Michael v CC South Wales (2015)'], 'the incremental approach is sensible but policy judgments still create uncertainty')])

T(5, 'Breach of duty: the reasonable person standard and risk factors', area='breach of duty',
  defs=[('reasonable person standard', 'The objective standard of care in negligence: what a reasonable person would have done in the defendant\'s position (Blyth v Birmingham Waterworks).'),
        ('Bolam test', 'A professional is not negligent if they acted in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of professional opinion (Bolam v Friern HMC), provided the opinion has a logical basis (Bolitho).'),
        ('res ipsa loquitur', '"The thing speaks for itself": where the thing causing harm was under the defendant\'s control and the accident would not normally happen without negligence, the court may infer breach.')],
  mcqs=[('In Nettleship v Weston (1971), a learner driver was judged by the standard of:', 'a reasonably competent qualified driver', ['a reasonable learner driver', 'her own ability', 'a professional racing driver'], 'Inexperience does not lower the standard.'),
        ('Which case shows that a low risk of harm may mean the defendant has not breached their duty?', 'Bolton v Stone (1951)', ['Paris v Stepney BC (1951)', 'Nettleship v Weston (1971)', 'Bolitho v City and Hackney HA (1997)'], 'Cricket balls had been hit out of the ground only about six times in 30 years.'),
        ('In Paris v Stepney BC (1951), the employer was in breach because:', 'it knew the worker had only one good eye, so serious harm was more likely', ['goggles were not available anywhere', 'the risk was unforeseeable', 'the worker consented'], 'Seriousness of potential harm.'),
        ('Which case shows that the social utility of the defendant\'s act can justify taking a risk?', 'Watt v Hertfordshire County Council (1954)', ['Latimer v AEC (1953)', 'Mullin v Richards (1998)', 'Wells v Cooper (1958)'], 'Firefighters rushing to save a life.'),
        ('In Mullin v Richards (1998), a 15-year-old was judged by the standard of:', 'a reasonable 15-year-old', ['a reasonable adult', 'a reasonable teacher', 'a reasonable professional'], 'Children are judged against the standard of a reasonable child of the same age.')],
  cases=[('Blyth v Birmingham Waterworks Co (1856)', 'a water main burst in an exceptionally severe frost and flooded a house', 'negligence is failing to do what a reasonable person would do or doing what a reasonable person would not do'),
         ('Nettleship v Weston (1971)', 'a learner driver hit a lamp post, injuring her instructor', 'a learner is judged by the standard of a reasonably competent driver'),
         ('Mullin v Richards (1998)', 'two 15-year-old girls were fencing with plastic rulers when one snapped and blinded one of them in one eye', 'a child is judged by the standard of a reasonable child of the same age'),
         ('Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee (1957)', 'a patient was given electro-convulsive therapy without relaxant drugs and suffered fractures', 'a professional is not negligent if acting in accordance with a responsible body of professional opinion'),
         ('Bolitho v City and Hackney Health Authority (1997)', 'a doctor failed to attend a child with breathing difficulties, and experts disagreed about whether attending would have helped', 'the professional opinion relied on must be capable of withstanding logical analysis'),
         ('Bolton v Stone (1951)', 'a woman outside a cricket ground was hit by a ball hit over a 17-foot fence, which had happened only about six times in 30 years', 'where the risk of harm is very small, a reasonable person may not need to take precautions'),
         ('Paris v Stepney Borough Council (1951)', 'a mechanic with sight in only one eye was blinded when a chip of metal flew into his good eye', 'where the potential harm is very serious, greater precautions are required'),
         ('Latimer v AEC Ltd (1953)', 'a factory floor became slippery after a flood, and the owners used sawdust but did not close the factory', 'the defendant need only take precautions in proportion to the risk; closing the factory was too costly'),
         ('Watt v Hertfordshire County Council (1954)', 'a firefighter was injured by a heavy jack on an unsuitable lorry used in an emergency to save a trapped woman', 'the social utility of the act can justify taking greater risks'),
         ('Haley v London Electricity Board (1965)', 'a blind man fell into a hole dug in the pavement which was guarded only by a hammer', 'the defendant must take account of foreseeable vulnerable people such as the blind'),
         ('Roe v Minister of Health (1954)', 'patients were paralysed by anaesthetic contaminated through invisible cracks in glass ampoules, a risk unknown at the time', 'a defendant is judged by the state of knowledge at the time of the incident')],
  ars=[('A learner driver owes the same standard of care as a qualified driver.', 'In Nettleship v Weston (1971) the court applied an objective standard.', 'A'),
       ('A defendant must eliminate every risk, however small.', 'In Bolton v Stone (1951) the cricket club was not liable because the risk was small.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain four risk factors the courts consider when deciding whether a defendant breached their duty.', 'Probability of harm (Bolton v Stone); seriousness of potential harm (Paris v Stepney); cost and practicality of precautions (Latimer v AEC); social utility (Watt v Hertfordshire); characteristics of the claimant (Haley v LEB).', '1 mark per factor with case, up to 4. [4]'),
        ('Explain how the standard of care applies to professionals.', 'Professionals are judged by the standard of a reasonably competent member of their profession (Bolam): not negligent if a responsible body of professional opinion supports their practice, provided it is logical (Bolitho); those doing DIY are judged as a reasonable amateur (Wells v Cooper).', '1 mark Bolam; 1 mark Bolitho; 1 mark further point. [3]')],
  cmps=[('Bolton v Stone (1951)', 'Paris v Stepney BC (1951)', ['Bolton concerned the low probability of harm, Paris the seriousness of the harm to a vulnerable claimant', 'the defendant was not liable in Bolton but was liable in Paris'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A} {who}. {B} was injured as a result.',
             slots={'who': [('is a learner driver who mounted the pavement while practising a three-point turn and hit {B}', 'judged as a reasonably competent qualified driver (Nettleship v Weston)', 'a competent driver would not have mounted the pavement, so there is a breach despite {A}\'s inexperience'),
                            ('is a 12-year-old who was play-fighting with a plastic ruler at school when it snapped and a fragment hit {B}\'s eye', 'judged as a reasonable 12-year-old (Mullin v Richards)', 'a reasonable 12-year-old would not have foreseen the injury, so there is probably no breach'),
                            ('is a surgeon who used a technique supported by a responsible and logical body of surgeons, although many other surgeons would not use it', 'judged by Bolam/Bolitho: a responsible, logical body of medical opinion', 'acting in accordance with a responsible body of opinion means there is probably no breach (Bolam)'),
                            ('is a surgeon who failed to check {B}\'s notes for allergies before an operation, a check every competent surgeon makes', 'judged by Bolam/Bolitho: a responsible, logical body of medical opinion', 'no responsible body of opinion would support this, so there is a breach'),
                            ('is an amateur who fixed a new door handle at home in the same way a reasonably competent amateur would; it later came off and {B} fell', 'judged as a reasonably competent amateur, not a professional carpenter (Wells v Cooper)', 'the work met the standard of a reasonable amateur, so there is probably no breach')]},
             issue='breach of duty and the standard of the reasonable person', wrong=['duty of care under Caparo', 'factual causation', 'volenti non fit injuria'],
             ask='Advise {B} whether {A} breached the duty of care.',
             law=['the standard is objective: the reasonable person (Blyth v Birmingham Waterworks)', 'learners judged as competent (Nettleship); children by age (Mullin v Richards); professionals by Bolam/Bolitho', 'risk factors: probability (Bolton v Stone), seriousness (Paris), cost (Latimer), utility (Watt), vulnerable claimants (Haley)'],
             apply=['{A} {who}', 'so {A} is {who_1}', '{who_2}'],
             concl='{A} is {who_1}; {who_2}'),
        dict(facts='{A} Ltd runs a {place} in {city}. {risk}',
             slots={'place': ['sports ground', 'factory', 'warehouse'],
                    'risk': [('Balls have been hit over the 5-metre fence only twice in 40 years; a passer-by, {B}, was hit by one.', 'low probability, so probably no breach (Bolton v Stone)'),
                             ('{B}, an employee known to be deaf in one ear, suffered serious hearing damage because {A} Ltd did not provide ear protection.', 'serious harm to a known vulnerable claimant, so probably a breach (Paris v Stepney)'),
                             ('After an unexpected flood, {A} Ltd spread sawdust on most of the floor but did not close the site; {B} slipped on an untreated patch.', 'reasonable precautions taken given the cost of closure, so probably no breach (Latimer v AEC)')]},
             issue='risk factors in breach of duty', wrong=['proximity in the Caparo test', 'novus actus interveniens', 'contributory negligence'],
             ask='Advise {B} whether {A} Ltd is in breach of its duty of care.',
             law=['breach is judged by what the reasonable person would have done', 'probability (Bolton v Stone), seriousness (Paris v Stepney), cost of precautions (Latimer v AEC), social utility (Watt), vulnerable claimants (Haley v LEB)'],
             apply=['{risk}', 'this points to {risk_1}'],
             concl='this points to {risk_1}')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the objective standard of care in negligence.', ['protects claimants consistently', 'insurance makes it fair to apply a competent standard (Nettleship)', 'risk factors give flexibility'],
       ['can seem harsh on learners and the inexperienced', 'Bolam favours professionals', 'risk factors make outcomes unpredictable'],
       ['Nettleship v Weston (1971)', 'Bolam (1957)', 'Bolitho (1997)', 'Bolton v Stone (1951)', 'Paris v Stepney (1951)'], 'the objective standard is justified, especially given insurance, though Bolam has been criticised')])

T(5, 'Damage: causation in fact, legal causation and remoteness', area='causation and remoteness in negligence',
  defs=[('but for test', 'The test of factual causation: would the claimant have suffered the damage but for the defendant\'s breach? (Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington HMC).'),
        ('remoteness of damage', 'The rule that the defendant is liable only for damage of a type that was reasonably foreseeable at the time of the breach (The Wagon Mound (No 1)).'),
        ('novus actus interveniens', 'A new intervening act of a third party, the claimant or a natural event that breaks the chain of causation between breach and damage.'),
        ('material contribution', 'Where the defendant\'s breach made a material (more than minimal) contribution to the damage, it may be treated as a cause (Bonnington Castings v Wardlaw).')],
  mcqs=[('In Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington HMC (1969), the hospital was not liable because:', 'the patient would have died from arsenic poisoning even with proper treatment', ['the doctor owed no duty', 'the patient consented', 'the damage was too remote'], 'Fails the but for test.'),
        ('Which case established that the defendant is liable only for damage of a reasonably foreseeable type?', 'The Wagon Mound (No 1) (1961)', ['Re Polemis (1921)', 'Hughes v Lord Advocate (1963)', 'Smith v Leech Brain (1962)'], 'Overruled the direct consequences test in Re Polemis.'),
        ('In Hughes v Lord Advocate (1963), the defendants were liable even though the explosion was unforeseeable because:', 'the type of harm (burns) was foreseeable, even if the precise way it happened was not', ['they intended the harm', 'the boys were trespassers', 'there was no break in the chain'], 'Precise manner need not be foreseen.'),
        ('In Smith v Leech Brain (1962), the defendant was liable for death from cancer following a burn because of:', 'the thin skull rule', ['the but for test failing', 'contributory negligence', 'volenti'], 'Take the victim as you find them.'),
        ('In McKew v Holland & Hannen & Cubitts (1969), the claimant\'s own unreasonable act of going down steep stairs without a handrail:', 'broke the chain of causation', ['was irrelevant', 'meant the defendant was strictly liable', 'was a thin skull case'], 'Unreasonable conduct by the claimant can be a novus actus.')],
  cases=[('Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington Hospital Management Committee (1969)', 'a night watchman with arsenic poisoning was sent home without being examined and died', 'there is no liability if the damage would have occurred anyway (but for test)'),
         ('Bonnington Castings Ltd v Wardlaw (1956)', 'a worker developed a lung disease from dust, some from a negligent source and some from an innocent source', 'a defendant is liable if the breach materially contributed to the damage'),
         ('McGhee v National Coal Board (1973)', 'a worker developed dermatitis after not being able to wash off brick dust at work', 'a material increase in the risk of harm can satisfy causation'),
         ('Fairchild v Glenhaven Funeral Services Ltd (2002)', 'workers developed mesothelioma after exposure to asbestos at several employers', 'in mesothelioma cases, each employer who materially increased the risk is liable'),
         ('The Wagon Mound (No 1) (1961)', 'oil spilled into Sydney Harbour and was set alight by welding sparks, burning a wharf', 'a defendant is liable only for damage of a type that is reasonably foreseeable'),
         ('Hughes v Lord Advocate (1963)', 'boys knocked a paraffin lamp left by workmen into a manhole, causing an explosion and burns', 'if the type of harm is foreseeable, the precise way it occurs need not be'),
         ('Doughty v Turner Manufacturing Co Ltd (1964)', 'an asbestos cover slipped into molten metal, which erupted unexpectedly due to a chemical reaction', 'harm from an unforeseeable kind of event (eruption rather than splash) was too remote'),
         ('Jolley v Sutton London Borough Council (2000)', 'a boy was injured when an abandoned boat he was repairing fell on him', 'it was foreseeable that children would play with the boat and be injured, even if the precise way was not foreseen'),
         ('Smith v Leech Brain & Co Ltd (1962)', 'a worker burned on the lip by molten metal developed cancer and died because of a pre-existing condition', 'the thin skull rule: the defendant takes the victim as he finds him'),
         ('Knightley v Johns (1982)', 'a police officer negligently ordered another officer to ride the wrong way through a tunnel after an accident', 'a negligent act by a third party can break the chain of causation'),
         ('McKew v Holland & Hannen & Cubitts (Scotland) Ltd (1969)', 'a man with a weakened leg after an injury at work fell when he chose to go down steep stairs without a handrail', 'unreasonable conduct by the claimant can break the chain of causation')],
  ars=[('A defendant is not liable if the harm would have happened anyway.', 'The but for test is used to establish factual causation.', 'A'),
       ('In negligence, the defendant is liable for all direct consequences of the breach.', 'The Wagon Mound (No 1) replaced the direct consequences test with reasonable foreseeability.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain the difference between factual causation and remoteness of damage.', 'Factual causation asks whether the breach caused the damage, using the but for test (Barnett); remoteness asks whether the type of damage was reasonably foreseeable (The Wagon Mound), limiting liability even where there is factual causation.', '1 mark factual + case; 1 mark remoteness + case; 1 mark relationship. [3]'),
        ('Explain the rules on remoteness of damage in negligence.', 'The damage must be of a reasonably foreseeable type (Wagon Mound No 1); the precise manner and extent need not be foreseen (Hughes; Jolley); the thin skull rule applies (Smith v Leech Brain); unforeseeable kinds of event are too remote (Doughty).', '1 mark per rule with case, up to 3. [3]')],
  cmps=[('Hughes v Lord Advocate (1963)', 'Doughty v Turner Manufacturing (1964)', ['in Hughes burns were a foreseeable type of harm though the explosion was not, so the defendant was liable', 'in Doughty the eruption was treated as a different, unforeseeable kind of event, so the damage was too remote'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A}, an employee of {B} Ltd, was injured at work in {city} when {acc}. {after}',
             slots={'acc': ['a guard rail that {B} Ltd had failed to repair gave way', 'a forklift driven carelessly by a colleague hit {A}', 'a ladder that {B} Ltd had failed to inspect collapsed'],
                    'after': [('{A} had a rare bone condition, so the injury was far more serious than expected.', 'the thin skull rule makes {B} Ltd liable for the full extent (Smith v Leech Brain)'),
                              ('At hospital a doctor gave {A} grossly negligent treatment that caused a new and much worse injury.', 'the negligent treatment may break the chain for the later injury (Knightley v Johns)'),
                              ('Weeks later {A}, still limping, chose to jump down a steep flight of stairs and broke an ankle.', 'the unreasonable act of {A} probably breaks the chain for the ankle injury (McKew v Holland)')]},
             issue='causation and remoteness of damage in negligence', wrong=['duty of care', 'breach of duty', 'vicarious liability only'],
             ask='Advise {A} on whether {B} Ltd is liable for all of the harm suffered.',
             law=['factual causation: but for test (Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington HMC)', 'intervening acts may break the chain (Knightley v Johns; McKew v Holland)', 'remoteness: reasonably foreseeable type of damage (The Wagon Mound No 1); manner need not be foreseen (Hughes)', 'thin skull rule (Smith v Leech Brain)'],
             apply=['but for {B} Ltd\'s breach, {A} would not have been injured', '{after}', 'so {after_1}'],
             concl='{B} Ltd is liable for the original injury; for the later harm, {after_1}'),
        dict(facts='{A} went to hospital in {city} with {sym}. The doctor negligently failed to examine {A} and sent {A} home. {A} later {res}.',
             slots={'sym': ['severe stomach pains', 'chest pains', 'a high fever'],
                    'res': [('died, and medical evidence shows {A} would have died even if treated immediately', 'fails the but for test, so the hospital is not liable (Barnett)'),
                            ('suffered permanent damage that prompt treatment would have prevented', 'satisfies the but for test, so the hospital is liable')]},
             issue='factual causation (the but for test)', wrong=['remoteness of damage', 'the Bolam test', 'contributory negligence'],
             ask='Advise whether the hospital is liable.',
             law=['factual causation: but for the breach, would the harm have occurred? (Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington HMC)', 'the hospital owes a duty and breached it by failing to examine, but breach alone is not enough'],
             apply=['{A} later {res}', 'this {res_1}'],
             concl='this {res_1}')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the rules on causation in negligence.', ['but for test is simple and fair', 'exceptions (McGhee, Fairchild) help victims of industrial disease', 'remoteness keeps liability proportionate'],
       ['but for test fails with multiple causes', 'Fairchild exception is uncertain and policy-driven', 'Hughes and Doughty are hard to reconcile'],
       ['Barnett (1969)', 'Bonnington Castings (1956)', 'McGhee v NCB (1973)', 'Fairchild (2002)', 'The Wagon Mound (No 1) (1961)', 'Hughes v Lord Advocate (1963)'], 'the rules are generally fair but exceptions show policy plays a large role')])

T(5, 'Pure economic loss and negligent misstatement', area='pure economic loss and negligent misstatement',
  defs=[('pure economic loss', 'Financial loss that is not consequential on physical damage to the claimant or their property, which is generally not recoverable in negligence.'),
        ('negligent misstatement', 'A careless statement of fact or advice that causes financial loss, recoverable where there is a special relationship (Hedley Byrne v Heller).'),
        ('special relationship', 'A relationship in which the defendant has special skill, knows the claimant will rely on the advice for a particular purpose, and the reliance is reasonable (Hedley Byrne; Caparo).'),
        ('consequential economic loss', 'Financial loss that follows from physical damage to the claimant or their property, which is recoverable (Spartan Steel).')],
  mcqs=[('In Spartan Steel v Martin (1973), which loss could the claimant recover?', 'Loss on the melt in the furnace and the profit on that melt', ['Loss of profit on all melts during the power cut', 'All pure economic loss', 'Nothing'], 'Loss of profit on future melts was pure economic loss.'),
        ('Which case first allowed recovery for negligent misstatement causing pure economic loss?', 'Hedley Byrne & Co v Heller & Partners (1964)', ['Caparo v Dickman (1990)', 'Spartan Steel v Martin (1973)', 'Murphy v Brentwood DC (1991)'], 'Though the bank escaped liability because of its disclaimer.'),
        ('In Caparo v Dickman (1990), the auditors owed no duty to investors because:', 'the accounts were prepared for shareholders to oversee the company, not for investment decisions', ['auditors never owe a duty', 'the investors were minors', 'there was a valid exclusion clause'], 'Purpose of the statement was crucial.'),
        ('In Chaudhry v Prabhakar (1989), a friend was liable for negligent advice about a car because:', 'he had claimed expertise and knew she relied on him', ['he was paid', 'he was a car dealer', 'the statement was fraudulent'], 'Special relationship in a social context.')],
  cases=[('Spartan Steel and Alloys Ltd v Martin & Co (Contractors) Ltd (1973)', 'workmen negligently cut a power cable, damaging a melt in a furnace and stopping production', 'consequential economic loss is recoverable but pure economic loss is not'),
         ('Murphy v Brentwood District Council (1991)', 'a house built on defective foundations had to be sold at a loss', 'the cost of repairing a defective building is pure economic loss and not recoverable in negligence'),
         ('Hedley Byrne & Co Ltd v Heller & Partners Ltd (1964)', 'a bank gave a favourable credit reference about a client, "without responsibility", which turned out to be wrong', 'there can be liability for negligent misstatement causing pure economic loss where there is a special relationship, though a disclaimer may prevent it'),
         ('Caparo Industries plc v Dickman (1990)', 'investors bought shares in a company relying on negligently audited accounts', 'a duty for misstatements requires that the defendant knew the statement would be communicated to the claimant for a particular purpose and relied on'),
         ('Chaudhry v Prabhakar (1989)', 'a friend who claimed knowledge of cars advised the claimant that a car had not been in an accident', 'a special relationship can arise in a social context where expertise is claimed and reliance is known'),
         ('Smith v Eric S Bush (1990)', 'a surveyor valued a house for a building society knowing the buyer would rely on it', 'a surveyor owes a duty to a house buyer of modest means who relies on the valuation'),
         ('White v Jones (1995)', 'a solicitor delayed preparing a new will and the testator died, so the intended beneficiaries received nothing', 'a solicitor can owe a duty to intended beneficiaries of a will')],
  ars=[('Pure economic loss is generally not recoverable in negligence.', 'Allowing recovery could open the floodgates to unlimited claims.', 'A'),
       ('A disclaimer can never prevent liability for negligent misstatement.', 'In Hedley Byrne the bank avoided liability because of its disclaimer.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain the requirements for liability for negligent misstatement.', 'A special relationship: the defendant possessed special skill; the defendant knew or should have known the claimant would rely on the statement for a particular purpose (Caparo); the claimant reasonably relied on it; and there is no effective disclaimer (Hedley Byrne; subject to UCTA, Smith v Eric S Bush).', '1 mark per requirement, up to 4. [4]'),
        ('Explain why the courts are reluctant to allow recovery for pure economic loss.', 'Fear of indeterminate liability (floodgates); economic loss is often better dealt with by contract or insurance; it would place an unfair burden on defendants (Spartan Steel; Murphy).', '1 mark per reason, up to 2. [2]')],
  cmps=[('pure economic loss', 'consequential economic loss', ['consequential loss follows physical damage and is recoverable, pure economic loss does not and generally is not', 'Spartan Steel shows both: the damaged melt and its profit were recoverable, lost future production was not'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A}, {who}, told {B} that {stmt}. {B} relied on this and {act}, losing {loss}.',
             slots={'who': [('a qualified accountant, in a formal meeting', 'a special relationship: expertise, known reliance for a specific purpose (Hedley Byrne; Caparo)'), ('a friend at a party who had no special knowledge', 'no special relationship, as the advice was casual and no expertise was claimed'), ('a friend who claimed to know a lot about cars', 'possibly a special relationship, as in Chaudhry v Prabhakar')],
                    'stmt': ['a company was financially sound and a good investment', 'a car had never been in an accident', 'a property was worth much more than its price'],
                    'act': ['invested in the company', 'paid a high price', 'took out a large loan'], 'loss': ['$20 000', '$45 000', '$8 000']},
             issue='negligent misstatement causing pure economic loss', wrong=['consequential economic loss', 'psychiatric injury', 'occupiers\' liability'],
             ask='Advise {B} whether {A} is liable in negligence.',
             law=['negligent misstatement causing pure economic loss is recoverable where there is a special relationship (Hedley Byrne v Heller)', 'the defendant must know the statement will be relied on for a particular purpose (Caparo v Dickman)', 'reliance must be reasonable; a social context can suffice where expertise is claimed (Chaudhry v Prabhakar)', 'a disclaimer may exclude liability subject to reasonableness (Smith v Eric S Bush)'],
             apply=['{A} was {who}', 'this suggests {who_1}', '{B} relied on the statement and lost {loss}'],
             concl='whether {A} is liable depends on a special relationship: here {who_1}'),
        dict(facts='Contractors working for {A} Ltd negligently cut a power cable outside {B} Ltd\'s {fact} in {city}. The power was off for {hrs} hours. {B} Ltd lost {stock} and also lost profits of {lp} because production stopped.',
             slots={'fact': ['ice-cream factory', 'bakery', 'steel works'], 'hrs': ['12', '24', '36'], 'stock': ['stock worth $15 000 that spoiled', 'a batch in production worth $8 000'], 'lp': ['$30 000', '$50 000']},
             issue='the distinction between consequential and pure economic loss', wrong=['negligent misstatement', 'remoteness in contract', 'Rylands v Fletcher only'],
             ask='Advise {B} Ltd on what loss it can recover.',
             law=['physical damage and consequential economic loss are recoverable', 'pure economic loss is generally not recoverable (Spartan Steel v Martin; Murphy v Brentwood)'],
             apply=['the {stock} is physical damage and recoverable, with any profit directly on it', 'the lost profits of {lp} from stopped production are pure economic loss'],
             concl='{B} Ltd can recover for the damaged stock and consequential loss, but not the {lp} of pure economic loss')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the law on pure economic loss in negligence.', ['prevents indeterminate liability', 'leaves allocation of risk to contract and insurance', 'Hedley Byrne gives a principled exception'],
       ['distinction between consequential and pure loss is arbitrary (Spartan Steel)', 'Murphy leaves house buyers unprotected', 'special relationship test is vague'],
       ['Spartan Steel (1973)', 'Murphy v Brentwood (1991)', 'Hedley Byrne (1964)', 'Caparo v Dickman (1990)', 'White v Jones (1995)'], 'the restrictive approach is justified by policy, but its boundaries are arbitrary')])

T(5, 'Psychiatric injury: primary and secondary victims', area='psychiatric injury',
  defs=[('primary victim', 'A person who suffers psychiatric injury and was in the zone of physical danger or reasonably believed they were (Page v Smith; Dulieu v White).'),
        ('secondary victim', 'A person who suffers psychiatric injury from witnessing harm or danger to another; they must satisfy the Alcock control mechanisms.'),
        ('recognised psychiatric illness', 'A medically recognised condition such as post-traumatic stress disorder or clinical depression, required for a claim; ordinary grief or distress is not enough.'),
        ('immediate aftermath', 'The period shortly after an accident, during which a secondary victim who comes upon the scene or victims may satisfy the proximity requirement (McLoughlin v O\'Brian).')],
  mcqs=[('Which case set out the control mechanisms for secondary victims of psychiatric injury?', 'Alcock v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire (1992)', ['Page v Smith (1996)', 'Dulieu v White (1901)', 'Hedley Byrne v Heller (1964)'], 'Arising from the Hillsborough disaster.'),
        ('In Page v Smith (1996), the claimant recovered for psychiatric injury although he was not physically hurt because:', 'he was a primary victim, as physical injury was foreseeable', ['he witnessed his family being injured', 'he was a rescuer', 'he had a contract with the defendant'], 'Primary victims need only show foreseeable physical injury.'),
        ('Which case allowed a mother to recover after seeing her family in hospital about two hours after a road accident?', 'McLoughlin v O\'Brian (1983)', ['Alcock v CC South Yorkshire (1992)', 'Bourhill v Young (1943)', 'White v CC South Yorkshire (1999)'], 'Immediate aftermath.'),
        ('In White v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire (1999), police officers who attended Hillsborough:', 'could not recover as primary victims because they were not in physical danger', ['recovered as employees', 'recovered as rescuers in all cases', 'recovered because of a contract'], 'Employees and rescuers must satisfy the usual rules.'),
        ('In Paul v Royal Wolverhampton NHS Trust (2024), the Supreme Court held that:', 'relatives who witnessed a death caused by earlier negligent failure to diagnose could not recover as secondary victims', ['all relatives can recover for grief', 'doctors owe no duty to patients', 'Alcock was overruled'], 'Medical negligence secondary victim claims were restricted.')],
  cases=[('Alcock v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire Police (1992)', 'relatives of people crushed at the Hillsborough football stadium saw events on television or identified bodies hours later', 'secondary victims must have a close tie of love and affection, be proximate in time and space to the event or its immediate aftermath and perceive it with their own unaided senses'),
         ('McLoughlin v O\'Brian (1983)', 'a mother saw her injured family in hospital about two hours after a road accident, before they had been cleaned up', 'a secondary victim who comes upon the immediate aftermath can recover'),
         ('Page v Smith (1996)', 'a man in a car crash was not physically hurt, but the accident revived his chronic fatigue syndrome', 'a primary victim can recover for psychiatric injury if some personal injury was foreseeable'),
         ('Dulieu v White & Sons (1901)', 'a pregnant barmaid suffered shock when a horse-drawn van crashed into the pub', 'a person put in fear for their own safety can recover for psychiatric harm'),
         ('White v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire Police (1999)', 'police officers who helped at Hillsborough suffered psychiatric injury', 'rescuers and employees are not automatically primary victims; they must be in physical danger or satisfy the secondary victim rules'),
         ('Chadwick v British Railways Board (1967)', 'a man spent hours helping at the scene of a horrific train crash and developed a psychiatric illness', 'a rescuer exposed to danger at the scene may recover'),
         ('Paul v Royal Wolverhampton NHS Trust (2024)', 'children saw their father collapse and die from a heart condition that hospital doctors had negligently failed to diagnose earlier', 'secondary victims must witness an accident, an external event; a death from an earlier medical misdiagnosis does not qualify')],
  ars=[('Secondary victims must satisfy stricter rules than primary victims.', 'The courts fear a flood of claims from people who witness harm to others.', 'A'),
       ('A person who sees a disaster on live television can recover as a secondary victim.', 'Alcock requires perception with one\'s own unaided senses.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain the Alcock control mechanisms for secondary victims.', 'A recognised psychiatric illness caused by sudden shock; a close tie of love and affection with the victim (presumed for parents, children and spouses); proximity in time and space to the event or its immediate aftermath (McLoughlin); perception with own unaided senses, not via TV or being told.', '1 mark per requirement, up to 4. [4]'),
        ('Explain the difference between primary and secondary victims.', 'Primary victims are in the zone of physical danger or reasonably believe they are (Page v Smith; Dulieu v White) and need only show foreseeable physical injury; secondary victims witness harm to others and must satisfy the Alcock control mechanisms.', '1 mark primary + case; 1 mark secondary + Alcock; 1 mark contrast. [3]')],
  cmps=[('a primary victim', 'a secondary victim', ['a primary victim is in physical danger, a secondary victim witnesses harm to someone else', 'secondary victims must satisfy the Alcock control mechanisms'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{B} negligently drove into a crowd at a festival in {city}. {A} {where} and later developed post-traumatic stress disorder.',
             slots={'where': [('was standing next to the crowd and narrowly escaped being hit', 'a primary victim, as {A} was in the zone of danger (Page v Smith; Dulieu v White)'),
                              ('saw {A}\'s own child being hit from a short distance away', 'a secondary victim who probably satisfies Alcock: close tie, proximity and own senses'),
                              ('watched the event live on television at home, knowing {A}\'s brother was at the festival', 'a secondary victim who fails Alcock because there was no perception with own unaided senses and the tie must be proved'),
                              ('arrived at hospital two hours later and saw {A}\'s own spouse there, badly injured and not yet treated,', 'a secondary victim within the immediate aftermath (McLoughlin v O\'Brian)')]},
             issue='liability for psychiatric injury', wrong=['pure economic loss', 'negligent misstatement', 'remoteness in contract'],
             iq='Which area of negligence is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {A} whether {A} can claim against {B} for psychiatric injury.',
             law=['a recognised psychiatric illness is required', 'primary victims in the zone of danger recover if physical injury was foreseeable (Page v Smith)', 'secondary victims must satisfy Alcock: close tie, proximity in time and space or immediate aftermath (McLoughlin), own unaided senses', 'rescuers must be in danger (White v CC South Yorkshire; Chadwick)'],
             apply=['PTSD is a recognised psychiatric illness', '{A} {where}', 'so {A} is {where_1}'],
             concl='{A} is {where_1}')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the law on psychiatric injury in negligence.', ['control mechanisms prevent floodgates and fraudulent claims', 'clear rules for primary victims', 'Paul (2024) gives clarity in medical cases'],
       ['arbitrary distinctions (TV v presence; two hours v longer)', 'close tie must be proved for siblings and friends', 'rescuers treated harshly after White', 'Law Commission (1998) proposed reform'],
       ['Alcock (1992)', 'McLoughlin v O\'Brian (1983)', 'Page v Smith (1996)', 'White v CC South Yorkshire (1999)', 'Paul v Royal Wolverhampton NHS Trust (2024)'], 'the law is driven by policy and is arbitrary in places; reform by Parliament is needed')])

# =====================================================================================
# 6 Occupiers' liability and nuisance
# =====================================================================================

T(6, 'Occupiers\' Liability Act 1957: lawful visitors', area='occupiers\' liability to visitors',
  defs=[('occupier', 'A person who has a sufficient degree of control over premises to be under a duty to those who come onto them; there can be more than one occupier (Wheat v E Lacon & Co).'),
        ('lawful visitor', 'A person with express or implied permission to be on premises, such as invitees, licensees, those with a contractual right or those entering under a legal right.'),
        ('common duty of care', 'The duty under s2(2) OLA 1957 to take such care as in all the circumstances is reasonable to see that the visitor will be reasonably safe in using the premises for the purposes for which he is permitted to be there.'),
        ('premises', 'Land and buildings, and also fixed or movable structures such as ladders, scaffolding, ships and aircraft (s1(3) OLA 1957).')],
  mcqs=[('Which case held that there can be more than one occupier of premises?', 'Wheat v E Lacon & Co Ltd (1966)', ['Roles v Nathan (1963)', 'Glasgow Corporation v Taylor (1922)', 'Haseldine v Daw (1941)'], 'The brewery and the managers both had control.'),
        ('Under s2(3)(a) OLA 1957, an occupier must be prepared for:', 'children to be less careful than adults', ['specialists to ignore risks of their trade', 'trespassers to enter', 'all visitors to be experts'], 'Glasgow Corporation v Taylor (1922).'),
        ('In Roles v Nathan (1963), the occupier was not liable for the deaths of two chimney sweeps because:', 'they were specialists who should have guarded against the special risks of their trade', ['they were trespassers', 'they were children', 'the occupier had no control'], 's2(3)(b).'),
        ('In Haseldine v Daw (1941), the occupier was not liable for a negligently repaired lift because:', 'it had reasonably entrusted the technical work to a competent independent contractor', ['the claimant was a trespasser', 'lifts are not premises', 'the claimant consented'], 's2(4)(b).'),
        ('In Phipps v Rochester Corporation (1955), the council was not liable to a five-year-old who fell into a trench because:', 'parents should not leave very young children unaccompanied in obviously dangerous places', ['the child was a trespasser', 'trenches are not premises', 'the council had given a warning'], 'Responsibility of parents.')],
  cases=[('Wheat v E Lacon & Co Ltd (1966)', 'a guest died falling down poorly lit back stairs of a pub run by managers for a brewery', 'there can be more than one occupier; control, not ownership, decides who is an occupier'),
         ('Glasgow Corporation v Taylor (1922)', 'a seven-year-old died after eating poisonous berries from a bush in a public park', 'an occupier must guard against "allurements" that attract children'),
         ('Phipps v Rochester Corporation (1955)', 'a five-year-old out with his seven-year-old sister fell into a trench on open land', 'occupiers can expect parents to supervise very young children'),
         ('Roles v Nathan (1963)', 'two chimney sweeps died from fumes after ignoring warnings while sealing a chimney flue', 'an occupier can expect specialists to guard against the risks of their trade'),
         ('Haseldine v C A Daw & Son Ltd (1941)', 'a lift repaired by specialist contractors fell, injuring a visitor', 'an occupier is not liable for faulty technical work by a competent independent contractor reasonably chosen'),
         ('Woodward v Mayor of Hastings (1945)', 'a child slipped on an icy step left by a cleaner at a school', 'an occupier remains liable where a simple task is done badly and could easily have been checked'),
         ('Darby v National Trust (2001)', 'a man drowned while swimming in a pond on National Trust land', 'there is no duty to warn of obvious dangers'),
         ('Laverton v Kiapasha (2002)', 'a customer slipped on a wet floor in a busy takeaway on a rainy night', 'the occupier had taken reasonable steps; the duty is to make visitors reasonably, not absolutely, safe')],
  ars=[('An occupier can discharge the common duty of care by giving an adequate warning.', 'Section 2(4)(a) OLA 1957 allows a warning that enables the visitor to be reasonably safe to discharge the duty.', 'A'),
       ('Only the owner of premises can be an occupier.', 'Wheat v E Lacon held that control is the test of occupation.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain how the common duty of care applies to children and to specialists.', 'Children: the occupier must be prepared for children to be less careful (s2(3)(a)), including allurements (Glasgow Corp v Taylor), though parents are expected to supervise young children (Phipps). Specialists: occupiers can expect them to guard against risks of their trade (s2(3)(b); Roles v Nathan).', '1 mark children rule + case; 1 mark parents point; 1 mark specialists + case. [3]'),
        ('Explain when an occupier is not liable for the work of an independent contractor.', 'Under s2(4)(b) where the occupier acted reasonably in entrusting the work, took reasonable steps to check the contractor was competent, and checked the work was properly done where possible (Haseldine v Daw); simple tasks may be checked by the occupier (Woodward v Mayor of Hastings).', '1 mark reasonable to entrust; 1 mark competent contractor; 1 mark check work. [3]')],
  cmps=[('a lawful visitor', 'a trespasser', ['a visitor has permission to be on the premises and is covered by the OLA 1957, a trespasser has none and is covered by the OLA 1984', 'visitors can claim for property damage; trespassers cannot'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A} Ltd owns a {prem} in {city}. {who}',
             slots={'prem': ['shopping centre', 'hotel', 'leisure park'],
                    'who': [('{B}, a customer aged 6 who wandered away from a parent, was injured after climbing onto a brightly painted machine left unfenced in a service area.', 'a child, so the occupier must expect less care (s2(3)(a)); the machine was an allurement (Glasgow Corp v Taylor)', 'likely to be liable, although the court may consider whether the parent should have been supervising (Phipps v Rochester)'),
                            ('{B}, an electrician called in to fix a faulty switchboard, received an electric shock from it while working.', 'a specialist who can be expected to guard against risks of the trade (s2(3)(b); Roles v Nathan)', 'probably not liable, as the shock was a risk ordinarily incident to an electrician\'s calling (Roles v Nathan)'),
                            ('{B}, an adult visitor, slipped on a spillage that had been left for two hours with no warning sign.', 'an ordinary adult visitor owed the common duty of care (s2(2))', 'likely to be liable for failing to make the premises reasonably safe, unlike the brief spillage in Laverton v Kiapasha'),
                            ('{B}, an adult visitor, was injured when a newly installed lift, fitted by a reputable contractor, fell suddenly.', 'an ordinary adult visitor owed the common duty of care (s2(2))', 'probably not liable if it chose a competent contractor and could not reasonably check the technical work (s2(4)(b); Haseldine v Daw)')]},
             issue='the common duty of care under the Occupiers\' Liability Act 1957', wrong=['the duty to trespassers under the OLA 1984', 'Rylands v Fletcher', 'private nuisance'],
             iq='Which area of law is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {B} whether {A} Ltd is liable under the Occupiers\' Liability Act 1957.',
             law=['s2(2) OLA 1957: the occupier must take reasonable care to see that the visitor is reasonably safe', '{A} Ltd is the occupier as it controls the premises (Wheat v Lacon)', 's2(3)(a) children; s2(3)(b) specialists (Roles v Nathan)', 's2(4)(a) warnings; s2(4)(b) independent contractors (Haseldine v Daw)'],
             apply=['{B} is a lawful visitor: {who_1}', 'on the facts {A} Ltd is {who_2}'],
             concl='{A} Ltd owed {B} the common duty of care; on these facts it is {who_2}')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the protection given to lawful visitors by the Occupiers\' Liability Act 1957.', ['clear statutory duty', 'extra protection for children', 'fair balance through warnings and contractor rules'],
       ['uncertainty about allurements and parental responsibility', 'warnings may not protect children or those who cannot read', 'occupiers may escape liability through contractors'],
       ['Occupiers\' Liability Act 1957 s2', 'Wheat v Lacon (1966)', 'Glasgow Corp v Taylor (1922)', 'Roles v Nathan (1963)', 'Haseldine v Daw (1941)'], 'the Act strikes a fair balance between occupiers and visitors')])

T(6, 'Occupiers\' Liability Act 1984: trespassers', area='occupiers\' liability to trespassers',
  defs=[('trespasser', 'A person who enters premises without permission or goes beyond the limits of the permission given.'),
        ('duty under the 1984 Act', 'The duty under s1(4) OLA 1984 to take such care as is reasonable in all the circumstances to see that the trespasser does not suffer injury on the premises by reason of the danger concerned.'),
        ('common humanity', 'The limited duty owed to child trespassers at common law before the 1984 Act, established in British Railways Board v Herrington (1972).')],
  mcqs=[('Under s1(3) OLA 1984, a duty is owed to a trespasser if the occupier:', 'is aware of the danger, knows or has reasonable grounds to believe the trespasser is or may be in the vicinity, and the risk is one against which protection may reasonably be expected', ['owns the land', 'has insurance', 'has put up any sign at all'], 'All three conditions must be met.'),
        ('Can a trespasser claim for damage to property under the OLA 1984?', 'No, only for personal injury', ['Yes, for all property', 'Only for cars', 'Only if the occupier was reckless'], 's1(8).'),
        ('In Tomlinson v Congleton Borough Council (2003), the council was not liable because:', 'the danger of diving into shallow water was obvious and arose from the claimant\'s own actions, not the state of the premises', ['the claimant was a child', 'there was no occupier', 'the lake was private property'], 'Obvious risks and personal responsibility.'),
        ('In Ratcliff v McConnell (1999), the college was not liable to a student who dived into a closed swimming pool at night because:', 'he was aware of the risk and accepted it', ['he was a lawful visitor', 'the pool was full', 'the college was not the occupier'], 's1(6): willing acceptance of risk.')],
  cases=[('British Railways Board v Herrington (1972)', 'a six-year-old was badly burned on an electrified railway line after getting through a broken fence', 'occupiers owe a duty of common humanity to child trespassers'),
         ('Ratcliff v McConnell (1999)', 'a student climbed into a locked college swimming pool at night and dived into the shallow end', 'a trespasser who knows of and accepts an obvious risk cannot recover (s1(6))'),
         ('Tomlinson v Congleton Borough Council (2003)', 'a man dived into a shallow lake in a park where swimming was banned and broke his neck', 'there is no duty to protect against obvious risks arising from the claimant\'s own voluntary activity'),
         ('Keown v Coventry Healthcare NHS Trust (2006)', 'an 11-year-old fell while climbing the outside of a fire escape', 'the danger arose from the child\'s own activity, not the state of the premises, so the occupier was not liable'),
         ('Donoghue v Folkestone Properties Ltd (2003)', 'a man dived from a slipway in a harbour at midnight in winter', 'the occupier had no reason to believe anyone would be there at that time, so no duty arose'),
         ('Rhind v Astbury Water Park Ltd (2004)', 'a man dived into a lake and hit a fibreglass container hidden under the water', 'no duty arises if the occupier did not know and had no reason to know of the danger')],
  ars=[('The duty owed to trespassers is lower than that owed to visitors.', 'Under the OLA 1984 a duty arises only if the conditions in s1(3) are satisfied.', 'A'),
       ('Trespassers can claim for damage to their property under the OLA 1984.', 'Section 1(8) limits claims to personal injury.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain the three conditions in s1(3) OLA 1984.', 'The occupier is aware of the danger or has reasonable grounds to believe it exists; knows or has reasonable grounds to believe the trespasser is in, or may come into, the vicinity of the danger; and the risk is one against which, in all the circumstances, he may reasonably be expected to offer some protection.', '1 mark per condition. [3]'),
        ('Explain how an occupier can discharge the duty owed to trespassers.', 'By taking reasonable steps such as warnings or discouraging entry (s1(5)); the duty does not extend to risks willingly accepted (s1(6); Ratcliff v McConnell); obvious risks from the trespasser\'s own activity do not create liability (Tomlinson).', '1 mark warnings/discouragement; 1 mark acceptance of risk; 1 mark obvious risks. [3]')],
  cmps=[('the duty to visitors under the 1957 Act', 'the duty to trespassers under the 1984 Act', ['the 1957 duty is to make the visitor reasonably safe, the 1984 duty is to take reasonable care that the trespasser is not injured by the danger', 'the 1984 duty only arises if s1(3) is satisfied and covers personal injury only'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A} Ltd owns a disused {site} on the edge of {city}. {know} {B}, aged {age}, climbed through a gap in the fence at night and was injured when {acc}.',
             slots={'site': ['quarry', 'factory', 'railway yard'],
                    'know': [('Local children often play there and {A} Ltd has been told about broken fencing.', 'knowledge of likely trespassers, so a duty may arise'), ('There has never been any sign of anyone entering the site.', 'no reason to believe trespassers would be present, so no duty may arise (Donoghue v Folkestone)')],
                    'age': ['9', '14', '25'],
                    'acc': [('a rotten floor gave way', 'a danger due to the state of the premises'), ('{B} dived into a flooded pit from a high ledge', 'an obvious risk from {B}\'s own activity (Tomlinson; Keown)')]},
             issue='the duty owed to trespassers under the Occupiers\' Liability Act 1984', wrong=['the common duty of care under the OLA 1957', 'private nuisance', 'vicarious liability'],
             iq='Which area of law is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {B} whether {A} Ltd is liable under the Occupiers\' Liability Act 1984.',
             law=['s1(3): duty arises if occupier is aware of the danger, knows or believes trespassers may be in the vicinity and the risk is one against which protection is reasonably expected', 's1(4): reasonable care to see the trespasser is not injured', 's1(5) warnings; s1(6) willing acceptance of risk (Ratcliff); obvious risks from own activity (Tomlinson; Keown)', 'only personal injury is covered (s1(8))'],
             apply=['{know_1}', 'the injury arose from {acc_1}', '{B}\'s age ({age}) is relevant to whether the danger was obvious'],
             concl='liability depends on whether s1(3) is satisfied: here there is {know_1} and the injury came from {acc_1}')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the protection given to trespassers by the Occupiers\' Liability Act 1984.', ['provides a basic level of protection, especially for children', 'reasonable balance with occupiers\' rights', 'warnings and fencing can discharge the duty'],
       ['courts emphasise personal responsibility (Tomlinson), limiting claims', 'child trespassers may not appreciate obvious risks (Keown)', 'no claims for property damage'],
       ['Herrington (1972)', 'OLA 1984 s1', 'Ratcliff v McConnell (1999)', 'Tomlinson v Congleton BC (2003)', 'Keown (2006)'], 'the Act gives limited but reasonable protection, rightly focused on dangers from the state of the premises')])

T(6, 'Private nuisance: unreasonable use of land and relevant factors', area='private nuisance',
  defs=[('private nuisance', 'An unlawful (unreasonable) interference with a person\'s use or enjoyment of land, or some right over or in connection with it.'),
        ('locality', 'The character of the neighbourhood, which affects whether an interference with comfort is unreasonable (Sturges v Bridgman).'),
        ('prescription', 'A defence where the nuisance has been actionable for 20 years without interruption; it does not apply where the claimant "comes to the nuisance" before it was a nuisance to them.'),
        ('abnormal sensitivity', 'The rule that a claimant cannot succeed if the harm arises only because of their unusually sensitive use of land (Robinson v Kilvert).')],
  mcqs=[('Who can sue in private nuisance, according to Hunter v Canary Wharf (1997)?', 'Only a person with a proprietary interest in the affected land', ['Anyone affected, including visitors', 'Only the local authority', 'Only owners who live on the land'], 'Tenants and owners can sue; family members without an interest cannot.'),
        ('In Christie v Davey (1893), the defendant was liable because:', 'he banged trays and shouted out of malice to disrupt music lessons', ['the music lessons were a nuisance', 'he had a 20-year prescription', 'he was a public authority'], 'Malice can make conduct unreasonable.'),
        ('In Coventry v Lawrence (2014), the Supreme Court held that:', '"coming to the nuisance" is no defence, and damages may be awarded instead of an injunction more readily', ['planning permission is always a defence', 'speedway racing is never a nuisance', 'only injunctions are available'], 'Flexible approach to remedies.'),
        ('Which case decided that a claimant cannot recover if the damage is caused by the abnormal sensitivity of their own activity?', 'Robinson v Kilvert (1889)', ['Leakey v National Trust (1980)', 'Halsey v Esso (1961)', 'Kennaway v Thompson (1981)'], 'Sensitive brown paper damaged by moderate heat.')],
  cases=[('Sturges v Bridgman (1879)', 'a doctor built a consulting room next to a confectioner\'s noisy kitchen machinery that had been used for over 20 years', 'locality matters ("what would be a nuisance in Belgrave Square would not necessarily be so in Bermondsey") and prescription runs only from when the activity became a nuisance'),
         ('Hunter v Canary Wharf Ltd (1997)', 'residents complained that a tower block interfered with television reception', 'only those with a proprietary interest can sue, and interference with TV reception by a building is not actionable'),
         ('Christie v Davey (1893)', 'a neighbour banged on the wall, shouted and beat trays to disrupt a music teacher\'s lessons', 'malicious conduct is likely to be unreasonable and a nuisance'),
         ('Hollywood Silver Fox Farm Ltd v Emmett (1936)', 'a neighbour fired guns near the boundary to stop silver foxes breeding', 'malice can turn otherwise lawful conduct into a nuisance'),
         ('Robinson v Kilvert (1889)', 'heat from a cellar damaged sensitive brown paper stored above it', 'there is no liability for harm caused only because the claimant\'s use is abnormally sensitive'),
         ('Halsey v Esso Petroleum Co Ltd (1961)', 'an oil depot emitted acid smuts, noise and smells at night in a residential area', 'emissions and noise causing physical damage and discomfort can be a nuisance'),
         ('Crown River Cruises Ltd v Kimbolton Fireworks Ltd (1996)', 'debris from a 20-minute firework display set fire to a barge', 'even a short-lived interference can be a nuisance if it causes damage'),
         ('Coventry v Lawrence (2014)', 'homeowners complained about noise from a nearby speedway stadium used for many years', 'coming to the nuisance is no defence; planning permission is not a defence; damages in lieu of an injunction may be awarded'),
         ('Kennaway v Thompson (1981)', 'a claimant built a house next to a lake used for motorboat racing, which later increased', 'an injunction may limit an activity rather than ban it'),
         ('Leakey v National Trust (1980)', 'a natural mound on National Trust land slipped onto neighbouring houses', 'an occupier who knows of a natural hazard must take reasonable steps to prevent it harming neighbours')],
  ars=[('Malice can make an otherwise reasonable use of land a nuisance.', 'In Christie v Davey (1893) noise made out of spite was held a nuisance.', 'A'),
       ('Moving to an area where a nuisance already exists is a defence.', 'In Coventry v Lawrence (2014) "coming to the nuisance" was held to be no defence.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain four factors the courts consider when deciding whether a use of land is unreasonable.', 'Locality (Sturges v Bridgman); duration and frequency (Crown River Cruises); abnormal sensitivity of the claimant (Robinson v Kilvert); malice (Christie v Davey); seriousness and nature of the harm (Halsey v Esso).', '1 mark per factor with case, up to 4. [4]'),
        ('Explain the defences to private nuisance.', 'Prescription after 20 years of actionable nuisance (Sturges v Bridgman); statutory authority (Allen v Gulf Oil 1981); but coming to the nuisance (Coventry v Lawrence) and public benefit are not defences.', '1 mark prescription; 1 mark statutory authority; 1 mark non-defences. [3]')],
  cmps=[('an injunction in nuisance', 'damages in lieu of an injunction', ['an injunction stops or limits the activity, damages compensate while letting it continue', 'Coventry v Lawrence encourages courts to consider damages more readily'])],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A} lives in a {area} of {city}. {B} {act}. {A} complains of {act_2}.',
             slots={'area': [('quiet residential street', 'a quiet residential area where such interference is less acceptable'), ('busy industrial district', 'an industrial area where some noise and smell are expected (Sturges v Bridgman)')],
                    'act': [('opened a late-night bar next door with loud music until 3 am every night', 'continuous, so more likely to be unreasonable', 'lack of sleep and stress'), ('held a one-off fireworks display that set fire to {A}\'s shed', 'short but caused physical damage (Crown River Cruises)', 'damage to property'), ('deliberately revved a motorbike outside {A}\'s window late at night to annoy {A} after an argument', 'malicious, so likely unreasonable (Christie v Davey)', 'being unable to sleep or enjoy the home')]},
             issue='private nuisance', wrong=['Rylands v Fletcher', 'negligence', 'occupiers\' liability'],
             iq='Which tort is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {A} whether {B} has committed a private nuisance and what remedies are available.',
             law=['private nuisance is an unreasonable interference with the use or enjoyment of land; the claimant needs a proprietary interest (Hunter v Canary Wharf)', 'factors: locality (Sturges v Bridgman), duration (Crown River Cruises), sensitivity (Robinson v Kilvert), malice (Christie v Davey)', 'coming to the nuisance is no defence (Coventry v Lawrence)', 'remedies: injunction, possibly limited (Kennaway v Thompson), or damages in lieu'],
             apply=['{A} must have a proprietary interest to sue', 'the area is {area_1}', '{B}\'s activity is {act_1}'],
             concl='{B}\'s conduct is likely to be a nuisance if, weighing these factors, it is unreasonable; {A} may seek an injunction or damages')],
  ev=[('Evaluate the extent to which private nuisance balances the interests of neighbours.', ['flexible reasonable-user test', 'factors such as locality and malice reflect fairness', 'Coventry v Lawrence allows damages to protect useful activities'],
       ['proprietary interest requirement excludes family members (Hunter)', 'outcomes are unpredictable', 'damages in lieu may let wealthy defendants "buy" the right to cause a nuisance'],
       ['Sturges v Bridgman (1879)', 'Hunter v Canary Wharf (1997)', 'Christie v Davey (1893)', 'Coventry v Lawrence (2014)', 'Kennaway v Thompson (1981)'], 'the tort balances interests reasonably, though access to it is limited')])

T(6, 'The rule in Rylands v Fletcher', area='the rule in Rylands v Fletcher',
  defs=[('the rule in Rylands v Fletcher', 'A form of strict liability under which a person who brings onto land and keeps there something likely to do mischief if it escapes is liable for damage caused by its escape, where the use of land is non-natural.'),
        ('non-natural use', 'An extraordinary or unusual use of land creating an exceptionally high risk to neighbours (Transco v Stockport MBC), not the ordinary use of land (Rickards v Lothian).'),
        ('escape', 'Movement of the dangerous thing from land occupied or controlled by the defendant to land outside it (Read v J Lyons).')],
  mcqs=[('In Read v J Lyons (1947), the claim failed because:', 'there was no escape from the defendant\'s land', ['the use was natural', 'the claimant consented', 'there was an act of God'], 'The explosion injured an inspector inside the factory.'),
        ('In Cambridge Water v Eastern Counties Leather (1994), the claim failed because:', 'the type of damage was not reasonably foreseeable', ['there was no escape', 'storing chemicals is a natural use', 'the claimant was a trespasser'], 'Foreseeability of the type of damage is required.'),
        ('According to Transco v Stockport MBC (2003), damages for which of these cannot be recovered under Rylands v Fletcher?', 'Personal injury', ['Damage to land', 'Damage to buildings', 'Damage to property on the land'], 'The claimant must have an interest in land.'),
        ('Which defence applied in Nichols v Marsland (1876)?', 'Act of God', ['Act of a stranger', 'Consent', 'Statutory authority'], 'Unprecedented rainfall caused artificial lakes to overflow.')],
  cases=[('Rylands v Fletcher (1868)', 'water from a reservoir built by the defendants flooded the claimant\'s mine through old shafts', 'a person who brings onto land something likely to do mischief if it escapes is strictly liable for the damage caused by its escape'),
         ('Rickards v Lothian (1913)', 'a water tap in a building was turned on by an unknown person and water overflowed into the floor below', 'ordinary domestic water supply is a natural use of land'),
         ('Read v J Lyons & Co Ltd (1947)', 'an inspector was injured by an explosion inside a munitions factory', 'there must be an escape from the defendant\'s land'),
         ('Cambridge Water Co Ltd v Eastern Counties Leather plc (1994)', 'chemicals spilled at a tannery seeped into water supplies over many years', 'the type of damage must be reasonably foreseeable'),
         ('Transco plc v Stockport Metropolitan Borough Council (2003)', 'a water pipe to a block of flats leaked, causing an embankment to collapse and expose a gas main', 'the rule applies only to an exceptionally high risk from an extraordinary use of land, and not to personal injury'),
         ('Perry v Kendricks Transport Ltd (1956)', 'a child was injured when other children threw a match into the petrol tank of an old coach', 'the unforeseeable act of a stranger is a defence'),
         ('Nichols v Marsland (1876)', 'exceptionally heavy rainfall caused artificial lakes to overflow and destroy bridges', 'an act of God is a defence')],
  ars=[('Liability under Rylands v Fletcher can arise without negligence.', 'The rule is a form of strict liability.', 'A'),
       ('A claimant can recover for personal injury under Rylands v Fletcher.', 'Transco v Stockport MBC (2003) linked the rule to interests in land.', 'D')],
  exps=[('Explain the elements of the rule in Rylands v Fletcher.', 'The defendant brought onto land and accumulated something; it was likely to do mischief if it escaped; it escaped (Read v Lyons); the use was non-natural (Rickards v Lothian; Transco); the damage was of a foreseeable type (Cambridge Water).', '1 mark per element, up to 4. [4]'),
        ('Explain three defences to a claim under Rylands v Fletcher.', 'Act of a stranger (Perry v Kendricks); act of God (Nichols v Marsland); consent of the claimant; statutory authority; default of the claimant.', '1 mark per defence with case, up to 3. [3]')],
  scen=[dict(facts='{A} Ltd stores {thing} in large tanks on its land in {city}. {cause} The {thing} escaped onto {B}\'s neighbouring farm, damaging crops and buildings.',
             slots={'thing': ['industrial chemicals', 'thousands of litres of fuel', 'toxic waste'],
                    'cause': [('A tank failed because of an unexpected fault.', 'no specific defence, so {A} Ltd is likely to be liable'), ('An intruder deliberately opened a valve at night.', 'the act of a stranger may be a defence (Perry v Kendricks)'), ('A storm of unprecedented severity damaged the tanks.', 'an act of God may be a defence (Nichols v Marsland)')]},
             issue='the rule in Rylands v Fletcher', wrong=['private nuisance based on noise', 'occupiers\' liability', 'vicarious liability'],
             iq='Which tort is most relevant?',
             ask='Advise {B} whether {A} Ltd is liable under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher.',
             law=['bringing onto land and accumulating something likely to do mischief if it escapes (Rylands v Fletcher)', 'non-natural use creating an exceptionally high risk (Transco)', 'escape (Read v Lyons) and foreseeable type of damage (Cambridge Water)', 'defences: act of stranger, act of God, consent, statutory authority'],
             apply=['storing large quantities of {thing} is likely to be a non-natural use', 'the {thing} escaped and damaged {B}\'s property, a foreseeable type of damage', 'on the cause: {cause_1}'],
             concl='{cause_1}')],
  ev=[('Evaluate whether the rule in Rylands v Fletcher still has a useful role.', ['provides strict liability for very dangerous activities', 'helps claimants who cannot prove negligence', 'Transco preserved the rule'],
       ['narrowed so much that it is rarely successful', 'overlaps with nuisance and negligence', 'statutory regimes regulate most dangerous activities', 'Australia absorbed it into negligence (Burnie Port Authority)'],
       ['Rylands v Fletcher (1868)', 'Read v Lyons (1947)', 'Cambridge Water (1994)', 'Transco v Stockport MBC (2003)'], 'the rule survives but plays only a limited role')])

